no seed list, no reference numbers are supplied by the brief.\n\n### 1.4 Python runtime\n- `python3 --version` → `Python 3.12.3`; `pip --version` → `pip 24.0 from /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages`.\n- **ML/NLP packages relevant to evaluation: none installed.** `pip list | grep -iE \"torch|transformers|sentence|vllm|llama|openai|sklearn|numpy|pandas|scipy|datasets|huggingface|tokenizers\"` returns zero matches. Only stdlib, plus `requests 2.31.0`, `urllib3 2.0.7`, `PyYAML 6.0.1`, `jsonschema 4.10.3` are available — none of these is useful for LLM inference, and all evaluation logic will therefore be specified to run on stdlib only.\n- Project dependencies are explicitly not preinstalled (per the environment note).\n\n### 1.5 Network status\n- `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\" https://pypi.org/simple/` returns `curl: (6) Could not resolve host: pypi.org` (DNS resolution fails). Confirms **network disabled** as stated in the environment note.\n- No subsequent fetch attempt was made (per the no-network instruction).\n\n### 1.6 Other observations\n- `$HOME` is empty (just `.` and `..`). `/home/actor` exists but is empty. No caches, no HF cache, no venv are pre-seeded.\n- No GPU (`gpu_enabled: false`); `cpu_affinity_count: 24` is reported but irrelevant given no inference will be run.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Operational Scope Confirmation\n\n**Choice: (b) a design that depends on a curated name-pair file generated procedurally inside `/workspace`.**\n\nJustification (2-3 sentences): The hypothesis is specifically about *real-world* celebrity parent-child relationships, so a fully synthetic (fictional-name) stimulus set would not actually test the claim. Because network is disabled, the name-pair file cannot be sourced externally; it must therefore be (i) authored as a small, hand-curated seed list of researcher-verified celebrity pairs, and (ii) augmented and validated by a deterministic in-workspace script that generates the full stimulus file (pair rows, distractor pools, fame-tier metadata, per-dimensional control columns) from that seed list. This keeps the design auditable, reproducible, and re-targetable without ever invoking the network. A small synthetic-surrogate arm is added only as a *feasibility* check for the scoring pipeline (not as evidence about celebrities), and is explicitly labeled as such.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Proposed Approach Outline\n\n**Stimuli and contrast.** The design uses a within-pair directional contrast on a curated set of *N* celebrity parent-child pairs (e.g., N ≈ 100–150 pairs, target size to be confirmed at approach_review). Each pair generates two trials: the **C→P** direction (\"Given child C, name C's parent\") and the **P→C** direction (\"Given parent P, name P's child\"). All other factors (closed candidate set, prompt template, decoding, scoring) are held constant across directions — the only manipulated variable is direction, which is what makes the contrast informative for the directional-bias hypothesis.\n\n**Models, querying, and units of analysis.** Because dependencies are not preinstalled and network is off, the design specifies a *model slot* rather than a concrete checkpoint: two open-weight instruction-tuned model families at comparable parameter scales (call them Model-A and Model-B for now; concrete choices deferred pending researcher input) plus a base/non-instruction-tuned reference from each family to test whether instruction tuning amplifies the asymmetry. Each trial is scored in a **closed-set k-way multiple-choice** format (k = 7: 1 gold + 6 distractor parent or child names, parallel across directions) presented with randomized distractor order. Units of analysis are: (i) **top-1 accuracy** per direction per model, (ii) **MRR per direction**, and (iii) a **Directional Asymmetry Index (DAI = Acc_C→P − Acc_P→C)** with a paired bootstrap CI over pairs and a **sign test** for a per-pair correct/incorrect flip. All metric definitions are specifiable purely in stdlib Python.\n\n**Candidate set and controls.** Distractors are sampled from a name pool built to match the gold entity on several prespecified dimensions — name frequency / fame tier (A / B / C, defined offline as bucketed Wikipedia-pageview tertiles from a baseline month, with tier definitions replicated in-workspace as a static table), gender of the queried person, era (pre-1980 / 1980–2010 / post-2010), and alias handling (each pair is tested under full legal name, common first name, and stage name where applicable). Additional strata handled explicitly: parents with a single child vs. multiple children (gold = most-associated child for fairness, with a pre-registered tie-breaking rule); deceased parents; half/step/adopted children excluded from the gold set but present in a labeled exclusion bucket; in-laws excluded entirely. Prompt framing is neutralized: the word \"celebrity\" or \"famous\" is **not** used in queries (to avoid scope-priming), the prompt template is parallel across directions, and a small parallel-prompt sanity check (e.g., neutral \"person X\" vs. role-cued \"the child of Y\") is included to detect prompt-cueing artifacts.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Assumptions and Uncertainties\n\n| # | Assumption | Source | Consequence if wrong | Mitigation |\n|---|------------|--------|----------------------|------------|\n| A1 | Directional bias hypothesis (C→P > P→C for celebrity parent-child) is real and worth investigating. | Public-task statement only; **not source-supported offline** (no citations fetchable). | Could be designing an experiment for a non-effect. | Pre-register DAI = 0 as the null; require a pre-specified minimum effect size and paired sign test before declaring support. |\n| A2 | A curated set of ~100–150 high-confidence celebrity parent-child pairs can be authored offline. | Assumption (researcher knowledge). | Small N or noisy gold labels reduce power and inflate label noise. | Procedural generator pairs each hand-written pair with (a) confidence flag, (b) two independent-verification requirement, and (c) a held-out duplicate-pair inter-annotator agreement check. |\n| A3 | \"Celebrity\" can be operationalized via an offline fame-tier table. | Assumption; defined statically as bucketed pageview tertiles from a fixed baseline month, replicated as a small in-repo table. | Tier definitions may not transfer to the model's training-cutoff world. | Sensitivity analysis: re-run DAI within tier-A pairs and within non-tier-A pairs; require consistency before claiming directional bias. |\n| A4 | \"Parent / child\" relationships are unambiguous in the test pairs. | Assumption; rules exclude half-siblings, step/adopted, in-laws, contested paternity. | Mislabeled gold reduces accuracy in both directions symmetrically (bias toward null, not toward the hypothesized direction). | Apply the same exclusion rules in both directions; report N_excluded and a sensitivity check that includes ambiguous cases. |\n| A5 | Prompt framing can be made directionally parallel without cueing the answer. | Assumption; standard practice in paired-prompt designs. | An asymmetric cue (e.g., role-naming) could manufacture apparent bias. | Parallel template audit; neutral-keyword version vs. role-cued version; require that the between-version discrepancy be small. |\n| A6 | Closed-set k-way MCQ with matched distractors is a fair scoring format. | Assumption; standard in LLM factual-recall benchmarks. | Possible; ceiling/floor effects, distractor-strength asymmetry across directions. | Pilot on a small calibration subset; require that distractor-pool difficulty is matched across directions by human-rated difficulty (offline rubric). |\n| A7 | Two comparable model families at similar parameter scale can be specified without listing exact checkpoints. | Assumption (design-only deliverable). | Deferring the choice blocks pre-registration of compute needs. | Researcher picks the two families at approach_review; debias by parameter count and training data overlap where possible (e.g., family pairs). |\n| A8 | All evaluation code is expressible in Python stdlib (no `numpy`, `scipy`). | Verified by `pip list` — neither installed and offline. | If a dependency is later deemed necessary, installation will fail. | Use stdlib `statistics` for CIs, a hand-rolled paired bootstrap over pairs, and a Wilcoxon-style sign test that requires only counts. |\n\n**Uncertainties explicitly flagged for the researcher to resolve at approach_review**\n1. **How source-supported is the directional-bias hypothesis?** — Not source-supported offline (no citations fetchable). The proposed design treats it as a falsifiable pre-registered hypothesis with a stated null, not as established prior.\n2. **How will \"celebrity\" and \"parent-child\" be defined and verified?** — Proposed offline operationalization in A2–A4 above; requires researcher confirmation or amendment (e.g., should we use a different fame proxy, or restrict to a single domain such as film/TV?).\n3. **How will prompt framing be controlled to avoid trivially cueing the answer?** — Parallel template + neutral-keyword vs. role-cued sanity check, as in A5; requires researcher sign-off on the exact wording at approach_review.\n\n## Proposed next step (no execution)\nAt `approach_review`, the researcher and I will refine: target N pairs, exact model family slots, exact prompt templates (neutral vs. role-cued), tie-breaking rule for parents with several famous children, and the precise pre-registered primary metric (proposal: paired DAI = Acc_C→P − Acc_P→C, with bootstrap 95% CI; sign test as the inferential test). Awaiting explicit approval before drafting the full experimental protocol.", "evidence": "**Repository inspection (executed exploratory commands, exit 0):**\n- `ls -la /workspace` → single file `task.json` (537 bytes).\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` and `-maxdepth 4` → only `/workspace/task.json`.\n- `find /workspace -name \"track.md\"` → no output (file absent).\n- `pip list | grep