{"id": "41078552-0322-4160-baa7-22cb479f5598", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:26:52.822416", "source": "user", "parent_id": "edecb41b-8d44-44ff-ba69-3cef10411dc6", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Inspection received and accepted: workspace contains only /workspace/task.json; track.md does not exist as a file (its `documents.track.md` field confirms the design-only constraint that repositories and compute are not required); no ML/NLP packages are installed; network is disabled (DNS failure to pypi.org). Scope option (b) is the correct call: a procedurally generated, researcher-curated celebrity name-pair file inside /workspace is the only path that is simultaneously real-world, offline, and auditable. The proposed contrast is methodologically sound: within-pair, paired, parallel prompts, closed-set k-way MCQ with matched distractors, closed-set scoring (top-1 accuracy + MRR), and a Directional Asymmetry Index (Acc_C→P − Acc_P→C) with a paired bootstrap CI and a per-pair sign test as the inferential test. The eight assumptions are honestly flagged, and items A2–A5/A8 are plausible on the supplied evidence. I will not approve yet because four choices remain open that will materially change the design's structure; please address only the items below and then re-submit. Do not draft the full protocol until I approve approach. Do not invent celebrity name lists in this round — that is for the next stage under the procedural generator. Specifically, please provide concise answers (one short paragraph each) to the following focused clarification questions, and update the corresponding cells of the assumptions table where relevant. (Q1) Model specification granularity. The proposal uses two 'Model-A / Model-B' family placeholders. For a design-only deliverable that pre-registers feasibility, what level of specificity is required: (i) named open-weight instruction-tuned checkpoints (please name up to two candidate pairs at comparable parameter scale, e.g., a 7–8B slot and a ~70B slot, or whatever scale you judge feasible), (ii) named model families with a lock-in procedure deferred to a pilot stage, or (iii) a hybrid where the design names e.g., one family concretely and leaves a second as a family-slot with explicit selection criteria? Whichever you choose, justify feasibility on this hardware (cpu_affinity_count: 24, gpu_enabled: false; compute/runtime budget not stated — please flag any hardware assumption you are making and how it constrains model size). (Q2) Base vs instruction-tuned contrast. Do you want the design to include a non-instruction-tuned base model from each family alongside the IT variant (4 model slots total, 4 comparisons), or restrict to instruction-tuned only (2 model slots, 1 primary family-pair comparison)? Including a base/IT contrast substantially strengthens the interpretive story (does instruction tuning drive the asymmetry, or is it a base-model property?) but doubles the design footprint; please tell me which to lock in. (Q3) Tie-breaking rule for parents with multiple famous children. Pre-register one rule now and we will reuse it in both directions: options include (a) most-associated child by a static offline fame-tier score, (b) firstborn, (c) eldest child who is independently verifiable as a 'celebrity' on the same tier table, (d) random among the top-2 with the random seed fixed. State the rule, its justification, and how it is applied symmetrically in C→P and P→C trials. (Q4) Fame-tier operationalization. Confirm that the offline bucketed pageview-tertile table (tier A/B/C from a fixed baseline month) is acceptable as the operational definition of 'celebrity' for inclusion AND for control stratification, or propose a substitute offline proxy (e.g., static curated lists such as A-list Hollywood actors, Grammy-eligible musicians, Forbes-celebrity-100 baselines) that you can hand-author inside /workspace without network. If you propose a substitute, specify how it will be built deterministically and auditable. After addressing Q1–Q4, please also append a short 'feasibility note' (3–5 sentences) summarizing (i) the compute/runtime budget you are assuming on the supplied hardware (24 CPU cores, no GPU, no network), (ii) the smallest N of pairs that would still make the sign test informative against the proposed primary metric, and (iii) any element of the design that becomes infeasible under these constraints and the proposed fallback. I will then approve and you may draft the full experimental protocol (rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility, pre-registration table).\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
{"id": "85fbaf9a-5d75-4a7e-81ea-e900abb475de", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:27:57.612399", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "41078552-0322-4160-baa7-22cb479f5598", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "# Clarifications Q1–Q4, Updated Assumptions & Feasibility Note (Stage: plan)\n\n> Reminder: design-only. No celebrity names invented, no experiment executed, full protocol deferred to next stage pending approval.\n\n---\n\n## Q1 — Model specification granularity\n\n**Choice: option (iii), a hybrid — name one concrete instruction-tuned family at a feasible scale, and keep the second slot as a family-slot with explicit selection criteria.**\n\n**Concrete recommendation (locked).** Slot-1 is named as a **7–8B-class open-weight instruction-tuned model** (e.g., *Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct* or *Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct* family — concrete checkpoint selected at lock-in). Slot-2 is a **family-slot reserved for a different vendor's instruction-tuned model at the same ≤8B scale** (selection criteria: comparable parameter count, distinct pretraining-token corpus from Slot-1 to mitigate same-family leakage, permissive license so weights can be re-cached offline; e.g., a Mistral-7B-Instruct-class candidate).\n\n**Hardware-feasibility constraint (flagged assumption).** `gpu_enabled: false`, `network: disabled`, `cpu_affinity_count: 24`. Without GPU and without downloadable weights, **the design assumes model weights are already present on local disk** (HF cache or vendor-provided snapshot) — without this, no LLM can be exercised at all and the experiment is reduced to a synthetic-surrogate simulation. On CPU-only inference, the practical ceiling for a closed-set k-way MCQ at k=7 with ≤50 output tokens is roughly **≤13B parameters at 4-bit quantization**; a ~70B model is **infeasible** on the supplied hardware within any reasonable runtime budget. Therefore the design locks model scale to **≤8B parameters**, not the 70B slot you mentioned. No compute/runtime budget was supplied by the brief; I am assuming a wall-clock budget on the order of **≤3 hours** for the full Slot-1 + Slot-2 run. Flagged as **Assumption A9**.\n\n---\n\n## Q2 — Base vs. instruction-tuned contrast\n\n**Choice: lock in the 2-slot IT-only design as primary; structure the base/IT contrast as an optional exploratory extension, NOT a pre-registered arm.**\n\n**Justification.** The public-task hypothesis is explicitly about \"LLMs\" — the artifact users actually interact with is instruction-tuned, so IT is the primary unit. Adding two base models doubles the design footprint and pre-registration surface, and base models answer free-form MCQ prompts with degraded calibration (less consistent format adherence), which is itself a confounding direction effect. The base/IT question is genuinely interesting (does IT amplify or create the asymmetry?), but it is a **secondary question** that can be answered post-hoc on the same stimuli by adding two base-model evaluations if budget permits. The pre-registered primary comparison is therefore **Slot-1 (IT) vs. Slot-2 (IT) on DAI**; the base/IT extension is exploratory and labeled as such in the analysis plan. Updated: **Assumption A7** is amended — the design fixes 2 IT slots, not 4 slots.\n\n---\n\n## Q3 — Tie-breaking rule for parents with multiple famous children\n\n**Locked rule: option (c) — \"eldest independently-verifiable celebrity child on the fame-tier table\".**\n\n**Definition (applied identically and symmetrically in BOTH directions).** For each parent P in the pair set, the gold child C\\* is determined by:\n1. Restrict P's children to those appearing in the static fame-tier table (Assumption A3 / Q4 below) at **any tier (A, B, or C)** — this is the \"independently-verifiable celebrity\" gate.\n2. Among that restricted set, select the **eldest** by date of birth (DoB taken from the hand-authored seed list).\n3. If no child passes the gate, the parent is **excluded** from the pair set (logged as `exclusion_reason = \"no_gold_child_in_tier_table\"`).\n\n**Symmetry.** The same C\\* is the gold for (a) the **C→P** trial (\"given child C\\*, name C\\*'s parent\") and (b) the **P→C** trial (\"given parent P, name P's child\"). This guarantees the gold is fixed by an **external selection procedure** that is not conditioned on either direction's expected difficulty and not conditioned on the model's likely competence — eliminating selection-on-the-outcome. Option (a) \"most-associated child\" is rejected because fame-association is itself plausibly correlated with model retrieval strength, which would conflate selection with the outcome. Option (d) \"random top-2\" is rejected because it adds variance without removing the fame-asymmetry confound. DoB is a fair tie-breaker because it is pre-treatment and plausibly independent of the experimental outcome variable.\n\n---\n\n## Q4 — Fame-tier operationalization\n\n**Choice: keep the bucketed-pageview-tertile IDEA, but operationalize it concretely as a hand-authored static table — and decouple *inclusion* from *stratification*.**\n\n**Concretely.** The brief did not provide an offline pageview dump, so a real bucketed tertile is not constructible offline. Substitute: a **single static file `/workspace/fame_tiers.json`** — hand-authored, deterministic, auditable — containing each celebrity's:\n- `name` (and `aliases[]` for stage names)\n- `tier ∈ {A, B, C}` with a tier-assignment rationale string per entry\n- `domain ∈ {film, music, sports, politics, business, other}`\n- `era` (pre-1980 / 1980–2010 / post-2010)\n- `deceased` (bool)\n- `dob`, `dod` where applicable\n\n**Tier-assignment rubric (offline, deterministic):**\n- **Tier A**: has held a head-of-state / Fortune-500-CEO-equivalent role, OR is a lifetime-achievement / hall-of-fame inductee in a major domain, OR appears on a hand-replicated Forbes Celebrity 100 baseline roster (researcher-curated subset).\n- **Tier B**: has received a major-award nomination (Grammy/Oscar/Emmy-equivalent) OR has had a top-charting release in a baseline year.\n- **Tier C**: has a durable public profile but does not meet A/B (e.g., recurring TV supporting role, mid-charting release, generational name recognition).\n\n**Separation of concerns.**\n- **Inclusion**: a pair is INCLUDED iff (i) parent and child can both be assigned to the same tier-or-better and (ii) the parent satisfies the Q3 rule resolving to one gold child, and (iii) the relationship is unambiguously biological (not step/adopted/half/in-law — excluded, logged).\n- **Stratification**: the tier is used **only to build matched distractor pools** within trials (e.g., a Tier-B parent receives distractors sampled from the Tier-B parent pool, matched on era and gender). Tier is **not** the inclusion criterion's only gate; inclusion additionally requires a researcher-curated high-confidence seed.\n\nThis is build-deterministic from researcher-seeded entries to a final `pairs.jsonl` file via an in-workspace procedural generator (deferred to the next stage). Updated: **Assumption A2** and **A3** are amended to reflect the static in-repo table and the inclusion/stratification separation.\n\n---\n\n## Updated assumption-table cells (only the rows that changed)\n\n| # | Updated assumption | Consequence if wrong | Mitigation |\n|---|--------------------|----------------------|------------|\n| A2 (rev.) | A curated set of ~100–150 high-confidence celebrity parent-child pairs can be authored **by the researcher into `/workspace/pairs_seed.jsonl`** at the next stage; the procedural generator then deterministically expands to the full `pairs.jsonl` (with distractor pools, tier metadata, and the Q3 gold-child tie-break applied). | Small N or mislabeled gold reduces power and inflates noise. | Procedural generator emits a `pairs_audit.json` with per-pair confidence flags, exclusion_reason, and an inter-coder agreement check on a 10% duplicate-pair subset. |\n| A3 (rev.) | \"Celebrity\" is operationalized via `/workspace/fame_tiers.json` (hand-authored, static, deterministic, with per-entry tier rationale); used **for stratification only**, while **inclusion** is gated by (i) tier-or-better for both parent and child and (ii) researcher confidence in the seed. | Tier definitions may not transfer to the model's training corpus. | Sensitivity analysis re-runs DAI within tier-A pairs vs. tier-B vs. tier-C; require consistency before claiming directional bias. |\n| A7 (rev.) | The design locks 2 IT model slots (Slot-1 = concrete 7–8B IT family; Slot-2 = distinct-vendor IT family at same scale). The base/IT contrast is included only as an **optional exploratory extension**, NOT pre-registered. | Deferring the IT/base question loses an interpretive dimension. | Open the option to add base-model evaluations post-hoc on the same stimuli, explicitly labeled exploratory. |\n| A9 (NEW) | **Local model weights are assumed pre-cached on disk** (HF cache or vendor snapshot), because GPU is disabled and network is off; otherwise no inference can run. Wall-clock budget assumed ≤3 hours for the full Slot-1 + Slot-2 run. | If weights are missing, only the synthetic-surrogate simulation arm can run. | If weights are absent, fall back to a deterministic rule-based surrogate (see Feasibility Note §iii) for at least the **pipeline feasibility check**, and explicitly report that real-model results are not produced. |\n\n---\n\n## Feasibility Note\n\n**(i) Compute / runtime.** The design assumes **24 CPU threads, no GPU, no network, ≤8B-parameter models at 4-bit quantization**, and a **≤3-hour wall-clock budget** for the full run. On this hardware, a 7–8B IT model in 4-bit should sustain roughly 5–15 tokens/sec, yielding ~25–50 minutes per model for ~150 pairs × 2 directions × ~7-choice MCQ (k=7) with ≤50 generated tokens per trial. Two IT models therefore fit comfortably in the assumed 3-hour budget, leaving headroom for one retry pass. A 70B-class model is **infeasible** on CPU without GPU within this budget (estimated 10–50× the 7B inference time), and is therefore explicitly excluded.\n\n**(ii) Power / N.** A paired sign test at α=0.05 (two-sided) on per-pair correctness gains adequate power (~0.80) with **N ≈ 50 pairs at Δ ≈ 20 percentage points** in DAI, and **N ≈ 100 pairs at Δ ≈ 12 percentage points**. I therefore propose **N = 100 pairs as the pre-registered minimum**, with N = 150 as the target if researcher time permits the seed list to extend that far. Maximum DAI detectable at N=100 with reasonable power is roughly 8–10 percentage points.\n\n**(iii) Infeasible elements and fallback.** (a) External model downloads are infeasible (network off) — **fallback**: the design specifies the model slot formally but reports that real-model inference requires pre-cached weights; without them, only a **deterministic rule-based surrogate** (e.g., a learned log-linear model on per-tier-frequency + n-gram features, calibrated against any single name a researcher can score by hand) is run, and this surrogate is explicitly labeled in the deliverable as a pipeline feasibility check, not as evidence about any LLM. (b) Large-scale prompt sweeps (e.g., ≥10 prompt variants × pair) are infeasible within 3 hours — **fallback**: lock to one primary neutral prompt template, plus two sanity-check variants (role-cued and terse), for a 3-prompt budget per trial. (c) External celebrity-verification fetches (e.g., Wikipedia ground truth) are infeasible — **fallback**: the ground truth gold set is the researcher-curated seed pairs themselves, with the procedural generator refusing to emit any pair not in the seed.", "evidence": "**Researcher constraints driving edits (from accepted message):**\n- \"do not invent celebrity name lists in this round\" → kept celebrity-name-dependent specifics deferred to next stage; only the file format, tier rubric, and inclusion/exclusion rules were specified.\n- \"scope option (b) is the correct call\" → preserved procedural-generator + researcher-seed architecture.\n- \"four choices remain open\" → Q1 (granularity), Q2 (base vs IT), Q3 (tie-break), Q4 (fame-tier).\n- \"Do not draft the full protocol until I approve\" → only clarifications + table edits + feasibility note; full protocol deferred.\n- \"cpu_affinity_count: 24, gpu_enabled: false; compute/runtime budget not stated\" → A9 created, hardware ceiling locked to ≤8B params, wall-clock budget flagged at ≤3 hours.\n- \"I will then approve and you may draft the full experimental protocol\" → plan remains at stage=plan with approach_approved=false.\n\n**Hardware-feasibility evidence (recap of earlier repo inspection):**\n- `gpu_enabled: false` per `task.json` environment.\n- Network: `curl https://pypi.org/simple/` → DNS failure (`Could not resolve host: pypi.org`).\n- `pip list | grep -E \"torch|transformers|vllm|llama|numpy|scipy\"` → 0 matches in current install; even if installed, 70B inference on CPU is not realistic in any budget a researcher would pre-register.\n- `python3 --version` → 3.12.3; stdlib-only analysis path remains feasible (`statistics` for CIs, hand-rolled paired bootstrap and sign test).", "assumptions": ["A2-rev: a researcher-authored `/workspace/pairs_seed.jsonl` of ~100–150 high-confidence celebrity parent-child pairs can be assembled offline and deterministically expanded by an in-workspace generator.", "A3-rev: \"celebrity\" is operationalized via a static hand-authored `/workspace/fame_tiers.json` with explicit per-entry tier rationale; tier is for stratification only, inclusion requires tier-or-better for both parent and child plus researcher confidence.", "A7-rev: 2 IT slots only as the pre-registered primary design; the IT-vs-base contrast is an optional exploratory extension post-hoc.", "A9: model weights are assumed pre-cached on local disk because GPU is off and network is disabled; the design is not executable as described if no weights exist, and the fallback is a deterministic surrogate pipeline-feasibility check.", "Wall-clock budget ≤3 hours is assumed for the full Slot-1 + Slot-2 run on CPU-only; not stated in the brief and therefore an assumption.", "Tie-break for multi-famous-child parents is \"eldest independently-verifiable celebrity child on the fame-tier table\" (Q3 option c); applied symmetrically in both directions so the gold is fixed by a pre-treatment rule not conditioned on either direction's difficulty."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the researcher will also exercise the optional base/IT exploratory extension (Q2 follow-up decision can still be made at lock-in).", "Exact checkpoint names for Slot-1 and Slot-2 (locked at lock-in step, not in this round).", "Whether N=100 or N=150 pairs is feasible for the researcher to seed; power analysis suggests N≥100 recommended, so uncertainty is about headroom, not feasibility.", "Whether the territory of \"biological parent / biological child\" definitions will leave at least 100 unambiguous pairs across the fame-tier table; some celebrity pairs may toggle on adoption, step-parents, or paternity disputes and must be excluded.", "Whether the assumption A9 (pre-cached weights) holds; if not, only the surrogate arm runs, and that constraint must be accepted by the researcher before proceeding."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-76809314-dd27-4bfb-b176-7323ed139e1b", "tool_call": {"id": "call-76809314-dd27-4bfb-b176-7323ed139e1b", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Address Q1-Q4 and add feasibility note\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"# Clarifications Q1\\u2013Q4, Updated Assumptions & Feasibility Note (Stage: plan)\\n\\n> Reminder: design-only. No celebrity names invented, no experiment executed, full protocol deferred to next stage pending approval.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Q1 \\u2014 Model specification granularity\\n\\n**Choice: option (iii), a hybrid \\u2014 name one concrete instruction-tuned family at a feasible scale, and keep the second slot as a family-slot with explicit selection criteria.**\\n\\n**Concrete recommendation (locked).** Slot-1 is named as a **7\\u20138B-class open-weight instruction-tuned model** (e.g., *Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct* or *Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct* family \\u2014 concrete checkpoint selected at lock-in). Slot-2 is a **family-slot reserved for a different vendor's instruction-tuned model at the same \\u22648B scale** (selection criteria: comparable parameter count, distinct pretraining-token corpus from Slot-1 to mitigate same-family leakage, permissive license so weights can be re-cached offline; e.g., a Mistral-7B-Instruct-class candidate).\\n\\n**Hardware-feasibility constraint (flagged assumption).** `gpu_enabled: false`, `network: disabled`, `cpu_affinity_count: 24`. Without GPU and without downloadable weights, **the design assumes model weights are already present on local disk** (HF cache or vendor-provided snapshot) \\u2014 without this, no LLM can be exercised at all and the experiment is reduced to a synthetic-surrogate simulation. On CPU-only inference, the practical ceiling for a closed-set k-way MCQ at k=7 with \\u226450 output tokens is roughly **\\u226413B parameters at 4-bit quantization**; a ~70B model is **infeasible** on the supplied hardware within any reasonable runtime budget. Therefore the design locks model scale to **\\u22648B parameters**, not the 70B slot you mentioned. No compute/runtime budget was supplied by the brief; I am assuming a wall-clock budget on the order of **\\u22643 hours** for the full Slot-1 + Slot-2 run. Flagged as **Assumption A9**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Q2 \\u2014 Base vs. instruction-tuned contrast\\n\\n**Choice: lock in the 2-slot IT-only design as primary; structure the base/IT contrast as an optional exploratory extension, NOT a pre-registered arm.**\\n\\n**Justification.** The public-task hypothesis is explicitly about \\\"LLMs\\\" \\u2014 the artifact users actually interact with is instruction-tuned, so IT is the primary unit. Adding two base models doubles the design footprint and pre-registration surface, and base models answer free-form MCQ prompts with degraded calibration (less consistent format adherence), which is itself a confounding direction effect. The base/IT question is genuinely interesting (does IT amplify or create the asymmetry?), but it is a **secondary question** that can be answered post-hoc on the same stimuli by adding two base-model evaluations if budget permits. The pre-registered primary comparison is therefore **Slot-1 (IT) vs. Slot-2 (IT) on DAI**; the base/IT extension is exploratory and labeled as such in the analysis plan. Updated: **Assumption A7** is amended \\u2014 the design fixes 2 IT slots, not 4 slots.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Q3 \\u2014 Tie-breaking rule for parents with multiple famous children\\n\\n**Locked rule: option (c) \\u2014 \\\"eldest independently-verifiable celebrity child on the fame-tier table\\\".**\\n\\n**Definition (applied identically and symmetrically in BOTH directions).** For each parent P in the pair set, the gold child C\\\\* is determined by:\\n1. Restrict P's children to those appearing in the static fame-tier table (Assumption A3 / Q4 below) at **any tier (A, B, or C)** \\u2014 this is the \\\"independently-verifiable celebrity\\\" gate.\\n2. Among that restricted set, select the **eldest** by date of birth (DoB taken from the hand-authored seed list).\\n3. If no child passes the gate, the parent is **excluded** from the pair set (logged as `exclusion_reason = \\\"no_gold_child_in_tier_table\\\"`).\\n\\n**Symmetry.** The same C\\\\* is the gold for (a) the **C\\u2192P** trial (\\\"given child C\\\\*, name C\\\\*'s parent\\\") and (b) the **P\\u2192C** trial (\\\"given parent P, name P's child\\\"). This guarantees the gold is fixed by an **external selection procedure** that is not conditioned on either direction's expected difficulty and not conditioned on the model's likely competence \\u2014 eliminating selection-on-the-outcome. Option (a) \\\"most-associated child\\\" is rejected because fame-association is itself plausibly correlated with model retrieval strength, which would conflate selection with the outcome. Option (d) \\\"random top-2\\\" is rejected because it adds variance without removing the fame-asymmetry confound. DoB is a fair tie-breaker because it is pre-treatment and plausibly independent of the experimental outcome variable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Q4 \\u2014 Fame-tier operationalization\\n\\n**Choice: keep the bucketed-pageview-tertile IDEA, but operationalize it concretely as a hand-authored static table \\u2014 and decouple *inclusion* from *stratification*.**\\n\\n**Concretely.** The brief did not provide an offline pageview dump, so a real bucketed tertile is not constructible offline. Substitute: a **single static file `/workspace/fame_tiers.json`** \\u2014 hand-authored, deterministic, auditable \\u2014 containing each celebrity's:\\n- `name` (and `aliases[]` for stage names)\\n- `tier \\u2208 {A, B, C}` with a tier-assignment rationale string per entry\\n- `domain \\u2208 {film, music, sports, politics, business, other}`\\n- `era` (pre-1980 / 1980\\u20132010 / post-2010)\\n- `deceased` (bool)\\n- `dob`, `dod` where applicable\\n\\n**Tier-assignment rubric (offline, deterministic):**\\n- **Tier A**: has held a head-of-state / Fortune-500-CEO-equivalent role, OR is a lifetime-achievement / hall-of-fame inductee in a major domain, OR appears on a hand-replicated Forbes Celebrity 100 baseline roster (researcher-curated subset).\\n- **Tier B**: has received a major-award nomination (Grammy/Oscar/Emmy-equivalent) OR has had a top-charting release in a baseline year.\\n- **Tier C**: has a durable public profile but does not meet A/B (e.g., recurring TV supporting role, mid-charting release, generational name recognition).\\n\\n**Separation of concerns.**\\n- **Inclusion**: a pair is INCLUDED iff (i) parent and child can both be assigned to the same tier-or-better and (ii) the parent satisfies the Q3 rule resolving to one gold child, and (iii) the relationship is unambiguously biological (not step/adopted/half/in-law \\u2014 excluded, logged).\\n- **Stratification**: the tier is used **only to build matched distractor pools** within trials (e.g., a Tier-B parent receives distractors sampled from the Tier-B parent pool, matched on era and gender). Tier is **not** the inclusion criterion's only gate; inclusion additionally requires a researcher-curated high-confidence seed.\\n\\nThis is build-deterministic from researcher-seeded entries to a final `pairs.jsonl` file via an in-workspace procedural generator (deferred to the next stage). Updated: **Assumption A2** and **A3** are amended to reflect the static in-repo table and the inclusion/stratification separation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Updated assumption-table cells (only the rows that changed)\\n\\n| # | Updated assumption | Consequence if wrong | Mitigation |\\n|---|--------------------|----------------------|------------|\\n| A2 (rev.) | A curated set of ~100\\u2013150 high-confidence celebrity parent-child pairs can be authored **by the researcher into `/workspace/pairs_seed.jsonl`** at the next stage; the procedural generator then deterministically expands to the full `pairs.jsonl` (with distractor pools, tier metadata, and the Q3 gold-child tie-break applied). | Small N or mislabeled gold reduces power and inflates noise. | Procedural generator emits a `pairs_audit.json` with per-pair confidence flags, exclusion_reason, and an inter-coder agreement check on a 10% duplicate-pair subset. |\\n| A3 (rev.) | \\\"Celebrity\\\" is operationalized via `/workspace/fame_tiers.json` (hand-authored, static, deterministic, with per-entry tier rationale); used **for stratification only**, while **inclusion** is gated by (i) tier-or-better for both parent and child and (ii) researcher confidence in the seed. | Tier definitions may not transfer to the model's training corpus. | Sensitivity analysis re-runs DAI within tier-A pairs vs. tier-B vs. tier-C; require consistency before claiming directional bias. |\\n| A7 (rev.) | The design locks 2 IT model slots (Slot-1 = concrete 7\\u20138B IT family; Slot-2 = distinct-vendor IT family at same scale). The base/IT contrast is included only as an **optional exploratory extension**, NOT pre-registered. | Deferring the IT/base question loses an interpretive dimension. | Open the option to add base-model evaluations post-hoc on the same stimuli, explicitly labeled exploratory. |\\n| A9 (NEW) | **Local model weights are assumed pre-cached on disk** (HF cache or vendor snapshot), because GPU is disabled and network is off; otherwise no inference can run. Wall-clock budget assumed \\u22643 hours for the full Slot-1 + Slot-2 run. | If weights are missing, only the synthetic-surrogate simulation arm can run. | If weights are absent, fall back to a deterministic rule-based surrogate (see Feasibility Note \\u00a7iii) for at least the **pipeline feasibility check**, and explicitly report that real-model results are not produced. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Feasibility Note\\n\\n**(i) Compute / runtime.** The design assumes **24 CPU threads, no GPU, no network, \\u22648B-parameter models at 4-bit quantization**, and a **\\u22643-hour wall-clock budget** for the full run. On this hardware, a 7\\u20138B IT model in 4-bit should sustain roughly 5\\u201315 tokens/sec, yielding ~25\\u201350 minutes per model for ~150 pairs \\u00d7 2 directions \\u00d7 ~7-choice MCQ (k=7) with \\u226450 generated tokens per trial. Two IT models therefore fit comfortably in the assumed 3-hour budget, leaving headroom for one retry pass. A 70B-class model is **infeasible** on CPU without GPU within this budget (estimated 10\\u201350\\u00d7 the 7B inference time), and is therefore explicitly excluded.\\n\\n**(ii) Power / N.** A paired sign test at \\u03b1=0.05 (two-sided) on per-pair correctness gains adequate power (~0.80) with **N \\u2248 50 pairs at \\u0394 \\u2248 20 percentage points** in DAI, and **N \\u2248 100 pairs at \\u0394 \\u2248 12 percentage points**. I therefore propose **N = 100 pairs as the pre-registered minimum**, with N = 150 as the target if researcher time permits the seed list to extend that far. Maximum DAI detectable at N=100 with reasonable power is roughly 8\\u201310 percentage points.\\n\\n**(iii) Infeasible elements and fallback.** (a) External model downloads are infeasible (network off) \\u2014 **fallback**: the design specifies the model slot formally but reports that real-model inference requires pre-cached weights; without them, only a **deterministic rule-based surrogate** (e.g., a learned log-linear model on per-tier-frequency + n-gram features, calibrated against any single name a researcher can score by hand) is run, and this surrogate is explicitly labeled in the deliverable as a pipeline feasibility check, not as evidence about any LLM. (b) Large-scale prompt sweeps (e.g., \\u226510 prompt variants \\u00d7 pair) are infeasible within 3 hours \\u2014 **fallback**: lock to one primary neutral prompt template, plus two sanity-check variants (role-cued and terse), for a 3-prompt budget per trial. (c) External celebrity-verification fetches (e.g., Wikipedia ground truth) are infeasible \\u2014 **fallback**: the ground truth gold set is the researcher-curated seed pairs themselves, with the procedural generator refusing to emit any pair not in the seed.\", \"evidence\": \"**Researcher constraints driving edits (from accepted message):**\\n- \\\"do not invent celebrity name lists in this round\\\" \\u2192 kept celebrity-name-dependent specifics deferred to next stage; only the file format, tier rubric, and inclusion/exclusion rules were specified.\\n- \\\"scope option (b) is the correct call\\\" \\u2192 preserved procedural-generator + researcher-seed architecture.\\n- \\\"four choices remain open\\\" \\u2192 Q1 (granularity), Q2 (base vs IT), Q3 (tie-break), Q4 (fame-tier).\\n- \\\"Do not draft the full protocol until I approve\\\" \\u2192 only clarifications + table edits + feasibility note; full protocol deferred.\\n- \\\"cpu_affinity_count: 24, gpu_enabled: false; compute/runtime budget not stated\\\" \\u2192 A9 created, hardware ceiling locked to \\u22648B params, wall-clock budget flagged at \\u22643 hours.\\n- \\\"I will then approve and you may draft the full experimental protocol\\\" \\u2192 plan remains at stage=plan with approach_approved=false.\\n\\n**Hardware-feasibility evidence (recap of earlier repo inspection):**\\n- `gpu_enabled: false` per `task.json` environment.\\n- Network: `curl https://pypi.org/simple/` \\u2192 DNS failure (`Could not resolve host: pypi.org`).\\n- `pip list | grep -E \\\"torch|transformers|vllm|llama|numpy|scipy\\\"` \\u2192 0 matches in current install; even if installed, 70B inference on CPU is not realistic in any budget a researcher would pre-register.\\n- `python3 --version` \\u2192 3.12.3; stdlib-only analysis path remains feasible (`statistics` for CIs, hand-rolled paired bootstrap and sign test).\", \"assumptions\": [\"A2-rev: a researcher-authored `/workspace/pairs_seed.jsonl` of ~100\\u2013150 high-confidence celebrity parent-child pairs can be assembled offline and deterministically expanded by an in-workspace generator.\", \"A3-rev: \\\"celebrity\\\" is operationalized via a static hand-authored `/workspace/fame_tiers.json` with explicit per-entry tier rationale; tier is for stratification only, inclusion requires tier-or-better for both parent and child plus researcher confidence.\", \"A7-rev: 2 IT slots only as the pre-registered primary design; the IT-vs-base contrast is an optional exploratory extension post-hoc.\", \"A9: model weights are assumed pre-cached on local disk because GPU is off and network is disabled; the design is not executable as described if no weights exist, and the fallback is a deterministic surrogate pipeline-feasibility check.\", \"Wall-clock budget \\u22643 hours is assumed for the full Slot-1 + Slot-2 run on CPU-only; not stated in the brief and therefore an assumption.\", \"Tie-break for multi-famous-child parents is \\\"eldest independently-verifiable celebrity child on the fame-tier table\\\" (Q3 option c); applied symmetrically in both directions so the gold is fixed by a pre-treatment rule not conditioned on either direction's difficulty.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether the researcher will also exercise the optional base/IT exploratory extension (Q2 follow-up decision can still be made at lock-in).\", \"Exact checkpoint names for Slot-1 and Slot-2 (locked at lock-in step, not in this round).\", \"Whether N=100 or N=150 pairs is feasible for the researcher to seed; power analysis suggests N\\u2265100 recommended, so uncertainty is about headroom, not feasibility.\", \"Whether the territory of \\\"biological parent / biological child\\\" definitions will leave at least 100 unambiguous pairs across the fame-tier table; some celebrity pairs may toggle on adoption, step-parents, or paternity disputes and must be excluded.\", \"Whether the assumption A9 (pre-cached weights) holds; if not, only the surrogate arm runs, and that constraint must be accepted by the researcher before proceeding.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-094907fd-90ba-4e11-b6fd-8918a161eae6", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 19}
{"id": "52756d29-0de3-4fe5-9e6a-1374a5a9b26e", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:27:57.615423", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "85fbaf9a-5d75-4a7e-81ea-e900abb475de", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-76809314-dd27-4bfb-b176-7323ed139e1b", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Handoff recorded; waiting for researcher review."}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "85fbaf9a-5d75-4a7e-81ea-e900abb475de", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
{"id": "fe4fd130-2535-4a21-b205-5e219c86c493", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:28:27.889258", "source": "user", "parent_id": "52756d29-0de3-4fe5-9e6a-1374a5a9b26e", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. You may now draft the full experimental design document. Constraints locked from this round: design-only deliverable (no execution, no fabricated results, no celebrity name lists invented); procedural-generator + researcher-seed architecture (option b); within-pair paired directional contrast (C→P vs. P→C) with closed-set k-way MCQ (k=7), matched distractor pools, and parallel neutral prompts; 2 instruction-tuned model slots (Slot-1 concrete 7–8B IT family, Slot-2 distinct-vendor IT at same ≤8B scale; exact checkpoint names deferred to a lock-in procedure that you must specify); base/IT contrast is optional exploratory only and explicitly NOT pre-registered; tie-break locked to 'eldest independently-verifiable celebrity child on the fame-tier table' applied symmetrically in both directions; fame_tiers.json is a hand-authored static table used for stratification only, with inclusion gated separately (tier-or-better for both members plus researcher confidence, and unambiguous biological relationship); CPU-only, no GPU, no network, ≤8B params at 4-bit, ≤3-hour wall-clock budget; pre-registered minimum N=100 pairs, target N=150; primary metric DAI = Acc_C→P − Acc_P→C with a paired bootstrap 95% CI over pairs and a paired two-sided sign test as the inferential test; fallback for missing weights is a deterministic surrogate labeled as a pipeline feasibility check and not as evidence. The deliverable must satisfy the submission contract ('Final experiment design with rationale, controls, analysis plan and feasibility') and should be organized with these sections, in this order: (1) Hypothesis and pre-registered null (falsifiable statement of the directional asymmetry, minimum detectable effect, α=0.05, β≤0.20); (2) Stimuli — the /workspace/pairs_seed.jsonl + /workspace/fame_tiers.json + procedural-generator pipeline that deterministically emits pairs.jsonl, distractor pools, and the pairs_audit.json file, including the schema for every column you will produce; (3) Models and querying — the model-slot spec, the lock-in procedure for the two IT families, decoding settings, hardware/runtime budget breakdown, and the offline weight-presence assumption with its surrogate fallback described as such; (4) Prompt and scoring protocol — exact primary neutral prompt template, the two sanity-check variants (role-cued, terse), response-parsing rules, k-way MCQ format, distractor sampling rule, randomization, and definition of top-1 accuracy and MRR; (5) Controls and stratification — fame tier, era, gender, deceased status, multi-child parents (with the locked tie-break), alias handling (full legal name vs. common name vs. stage name), and explicit exclusion buckets (step/adopted/half/in-law/contested) with logging fields; (6) Analysis plan — DAI definition, paired bootstrap CI over pairs with B=10,000 (or specify B given CPU budget), paired two-sided sign test as the primary inferential test, per-tier sensitivity analyses, per-prompt-variant sanity checks (require small between-variant discrepancy), and pre-registered decision rules (when is the hypothesis supported, when is it refuted, when is it inconclusive); (7) Pre-registration table — a single table enumerating every pre-registered choice (model slots, scale, N, k, prompt template, tie-break, distractor rule, primary metric, CI method, inferential test, α, minimum effect size, sensitivity analyses) so the reader can audit what was fixed before seeing results; (8) Feasibility — restate the ≤8B parameter ceiling, the ≤3-hour wall-clock budget, the power analysis showing N=100 is informative at Δ≈12 pp, the explicit infeasibility of 70B-class models and of large prompt sweeps on this hardware, and the surrogate fallback; (9) Threats to validity and limitations — including the assumption-locality problem (no offline citation could source-verify the hypothesis, so it is treated as falsifiable pre-registered prior), unmodeled distractor-difficulty asymmetry, potential prompt-cueing artifacts, and selection effects from the researcher-curated seed. End the document with a one-paragraph 'what would change if executed' note that acknowledges the design-only constraint. Do not invent celebrity names, do not invent numeric results, do not claim the experiment ran. Return the full protocol as a single Markdown document.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 21}
{"id": "39ee53e9-f360-435e-b783-fc549407cb15", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:30:47.203704", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "fe4fd130-2535-4a21-b205-5e219c86c493", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experimental Design — Directional Asymmetry in Celebrity Parent–Child Retrieval by LLMs (Design-Only)\n\n> **Scope.** This document is a complete, offline, design-only experimental protocol. No experiment has been executed. No celebrity names, no results, no reference numbers have been fabricated. Where the protocol depends on researcher input (seed lists, model checkpoints), the dependency is marked explicitly and the lock-in procedure is specified.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Hypothesis and Pre-registered Null\n\n**H1 (directional asymmetry).** For a held-out set of celebrity parent–child pairs, an instruction-tuned LLM achieves higher top-1 accuracy when asked to retrieve a **parent given a child** (Child→Parent; C→P) than when asked to retrieve a **child given a parent** (Parent→Child; P→C).\n\nFormally, with the **Directional Asymmetry Index**\n\n> **DAI := Acc_C→P − Acc_P→C**\n\nthe pre-registered directional hypothesis is\n\n> **H1: DAI > 0**\n\n**H0 (null).**\n\n> **H0: DAI = 0**\n\ni.e., no direction effect: parents and children are equally retrievable in their respective directions.\n\n**Pre-registered statistical targets.**\n- **Inferential test:** paired two-sided **sign test** on per-pair correct/incorrect outcomes across directions (α = 0.05, two-sided).\n- **Effect estimate:** paired bootstrap CI for DAI over pairs (B = 10,000 resamples, pair as the resampling unit); 95% percentile interval.\n- **Power target:** β ≤ 0.20 (i.e., power ≥ 0.80) for detecting a within-pair direction flip rate corresponding to **Δ ≈ 12 percentage points** in DAI.\n- **Minimum pre-registered sample size:** **N = 100 pairs**. Target N = 150 pairs if seed authoring permits.\n- **Decision rule (see §6):** *supported* requires the sign test p < 0.05 with the bootstrap CI for DAI excluding 0 and DAI > 0; *refuted* is the mirror of supported with DAI < 0; *inconclusive* otherwise. The hypotheses are intentionally a **falsifiable pre-registered prior**, not a source-supported prior (see §9 — assumption locality).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Stimuli — Files and Procedural Generator\n\nAll stimulus construction is offline, deterministic, and fully reproducible from researcher-authored seeds. No external data is fetched.\n\n### 2.1 File layout in `/workspace`\n\n| Path | Author | Role |\n|------|--------|------|\n| `/workspace/fame_tiers.json` | Researcher (hand-authored, deferred) | Static tier table used for **stratification only**. |\n| `/workspace/pairs_seed.jsonl` | Researcher (hand-authored, deferred) | High-confidence parent–child pairs. |\n| `/workspace/generate_pairs.py` | This protocol | Deterministic procedural generator. |\n| `/workspace/pairs.jsonl` | Generator output | Final analysis-ready pair list. |\n| `/workspace/pairs_audit.json` | Generator output | Audit / provenance log. |\n| `/workspace/distractor_pools.json` | Generator output | Pre-built matched distractor pools per tier-pool. |\n| `/workspace/master_seed.txt` | Researcher lock-in | 64-hex fixed seed; pins all randomness in the pipeline. |\n| `/workspace/PRE_REGISTRATION.md` | Lock-in artifact | Frozen pre-registration decisions (dumped from §7). |\n\n### 2.2 `fame_tiers.json` schema (stratification table)\n\nEach entry is a single object with:\n- `name` (string, canonical full name).\n- `aliases[]` (list of strings; e.g., stage names, before/after marriage names).\n- `gender` ∈ {`F`, `M`, `NB`, `UNK`}.\n- `tier` ∈ {`A`, `B`, `C`} with `tier_rationale` (one short clause, hand-authored).\n- `domain` ∈ {`film`, `music`, `sports`, `politics`, `business`, `royalty`, `other`}.\n- `era` ∈ {`pre_1980`, `1980_2010`, `post_2010`}.\n- `deceased` (bool) and `dob`, `dod` (ISO dates; `null` if unknown).\n\nInclusion in the tier table requires at minimum a viable `tier_rationale`. Tier assignment rubric (offline, deterministic, lock-in):\n- **Tier A.** Held a top-tier public leadership role in a baseline reference year (e.g., Forbes Celebrity 100 baseline), OR head-of-state, OR lifetime / hall-of-fame inductee of a major domain.\n- **Tier B.** Major-award nominee or winner (Grammy/Oscar/Emmy-equivalent) OR a top-charting release in a baseline year OR recurring leading role in a flagship series.\n- **Tier C.** Durable public profile but not meeting A or B (e.g., mid-tier release, supporting roles, generational recognition by other criteria).\n\nInclusion into the **pair set** is *not* gated by tier alone (see §5.1). Tier only governs distractor-pool sampling and per-tier sensitivity strata.\n\n### 2.3 `pairs_seed.jsonl` schema (researcher-authored)\n\nOne JSON object per line:\n- `pair_id` (string, `p0001`, …).\n- `parent_name` (string, must match a `name` in `fame_tiers.json`).\n- `gold_child_name` (string, must match a `name` in `fame_tiers.json`; the resolution rule in §5.5 below is also enforced by the generator as a cross-check).\n- `relationship` must equal `\"biological\"` (any other value triggers exclusion; see §5.6).\n- `confidence` ∈ {1,2,3,4,5} (researcher self-rated; pairs with `confidence < 4` are flagged for audit, not silently dropped).\n- `parent_dob`, `child_dob` (ISO date).\n- `notes` (free text; e.g., references to ambiguous paternity — these pairs are still excluded by §5.6).\n\n### 2.4 `generate_pairs.py` — deterministic procedural generator\n\nThe generator reads the two seed files and `master_seed.txt` and emits three artifacts. Pseudocode:\n\n```\nseeds   = read_jsonl(\"pairs_seed.jsonl\")\ntiers   = read_json(\"fame_tiers.json\")\nrng     = seeded_rng(\"master_seed.txt\")\n\n# 1. Apply inclusion gates.\nfor s in seeds:\n    if s.relationship != \"biological\":  exclude(reason=\"non_biological\")\n    if s.parent_name not in tiers:      exclude(reason=\"parent_not_in_tier_table\")\n    if s.gold_child_name not in tiers:  exclude(reason=\"child_not_in_tier_table\")\n    if min(parent_tier, child_tier) == \"C\" and s.confidence < 5:\n        exclude(reason=\"tier_C_requires_top_confidence\")\n\n# 2. Apply Q3 gold-child tie-break (defensive cross-check).\n#    Gold child must be the eldest of parent's children who are in fame_tiers.json.\n#    If the seed's gold_child_name disagrees with the deterministic eldest-in-table\n#    child, log the disagreement and prefer the deterministic choice (logged, not silent).\n\n# 3. Pre-build distractor pools by (role, era, tier, gender).\nbuild_distractor_pools(tiers, rng) -> \"distractor_pools.json\"\n\n# 4. Emit analysis-ready pairs.\nemit_pairs_jsonl(...)\nemit_audit_json(...)\n```\n\n### 2.5 `pairs.jsonl` schema (analysis-ready)\n\n- `pair_id`\n- `parent_name`, `parent_gender`, `parent_tier`, `parent_era`, `parent_deceased`\n- `gold_child_name`, `child_gender`, `child_tier`, `child_era`, `child_deceased`\n- `aliases_parent[]`, `aliases_child[]`\n- `relationship` ∈ {`biological`}\n- `inclusion_reason` (e.g., `\"both_tier_or_better\"`, `\"tier_C_top_confidence\"`)\n- `exclusion_reason` (only present if excluded; otherwise `null`)\n- `tier_pool` (the (era × tier × role) stratum used for distractor sampling)\n- `prompt_variant_seed` (fixed hash for trial-level randomness)\n- `gold_tiebreak_method` (`\"eldest_in_table\"`, always)\n\n### 2.6 `pairs_audit.json` schema (provenance)\n\n- `generator_version` (semver, frozen)\n- `master_seed_sha256`\n- `seeds_in`, `seeds_out_after_gates`\n- `tier_distribution` (histogram `{A,B,C}` for parents and children)\n- `era_distribution` (histogram for parents and children)\n- `gender_cross_table` (parent_gender × child_gender counts)\n- `exclusion_histogram` (counts by `exclusion_reason`)\n- `inter_pool_distractor_count_min`, `_max` (distractor-pool sizes; detect empty pools)\n- `tiebreak_disagreements` (list of seeds where seed gold ≠ deterministic eldest-in-table; kept but resolved to deterministic)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Models and Querying\n\n### 3.1 Model slots (pre-registered, primary)\n\n| Slot | Role | Scale | Status |\n|------|------|-------|--------|\n| **Slot-1** | Concrete instruction-tuned family (deferred checkpoint) | ≤ 8B params, 4-bit | **Pre-registered** |\n| **Slot-2** | Distinct-vendor instruction-tuned family at the same scale | ≤ 8B params, 4-bit | **Pre-registered** |\n\nConcretely, Slot-1 is named as a candidate **Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct**-class family and Slot-2 as a candidate **Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct**-class family. **Final checkpoint choice is deferred to the lock-in procedure (see §3.3).** The pair is selected to (a) be at ≤8B parameter scale for CPU feasibility, (b) come from distinct pretraining-token corpora to mitigate same-family leakage, and (c) be released under a permissive license so weights can be redistributed offline.\n\n### 3.2 Decoding settings\n\n- `temperature = 0.0` (greedy; deterministic; reproducibility).\n- `max_new_tokens = 8` (a single letter response plus stopping margin).\n- `repetition_penalty = 1.0` (no penalty; MCQ format leaves no role for repetition).\n- `do_sample = False`.\n- Quantization: 4-bit weight loading via `bitsandbytes`-style scheme if available; pure FP16 fallback documented.\n- Context: prompt only (≤ 600 tokens), no chat-template tokens added that would shift presented-options order.\n- Batching: trial-level `batch_size = 1`; parallel trials fanned out across `cpu_affinity_count = 24` worker processes.\n\n### 3.3 Lock-in procedure for exact checkpoints\n\nBecause checkpoints cannot be downloaded (network disabled) and weights cannot pre-exist by construction of this design, the lock-in is a *human-in-the-loop* procedure:\n\n1. **Step L1.** Researcher places pre-cached weight directories for **both** slots at `/workspace/models/<slot_id>/` (e.g., `/workspace/models/slot1/snapshots/…`).\n2. **Step L2.** Researcher runs `lock_in_check.py`, which:\n    - asserts the directory exists and `/workspace/models/slot1/config.json` declares `hidden_size` and parameter count ≤ 8B;\n    - asserts there exists at least one file matching `*.safetensors` or `*.bin`;\n    - prints the model's `model_type`, parameter count, and SHA-256 of `config.json`;\n    - prompts the researcher to type `LOCK <slot_id>` on stderr to confirm.\n3. **Step L3.** On LOCK, the lock-in script appends the model's identifier and SHA-256 to `PRE_REGISTRATION.md` and freezes the slot. After lock-in, no slot substitution is permitted without a re-pre-registration entry.\n4. **Step L4.** The same procedure is repeated independently for Slot-2.\n\nIf lock-in fails for a slot, the slot is reported as **unrunnable** and the surrogate fallback (§3.5) is engaged for that slot only.\n\n### 3.4 Hardware-runtime budget breakdown\n\n| Phase | Estimated wall-clock (24 CPUs, no GPU, 4-bit) |\n|-------|---------------------------------------------|\n| Lock-in check (L1–L4) | < 1 minute (file enumeration only). |\n| Generator (deterministic) | < 30 seconds. |\n| Per-model MCQ scoring, N=100 pairs × 2 directions | ~20–40 minutes (≈ 5–15 tok/sec, ≤8B params, 4-bit). |\n| Per-model MCQ scoring, prompt-variant arms (×2 variants × 2 directions × N) | ~40–80 minutes. |\n| Analysis (bootstrap, sign tests, sensitivity) | < 5 minutes (stdlib only; B=10,000 over N=100). |\n| **Total for both IT slots, single prompt** | ~1–1.5 hours. |\n| **Total for both IT slots, primary + 2 prompt variants** | ~2.5–3 hours. |\n\nThe pre-registered budget is **≤ 3 hours wall-clock**, leaving the prompt-variant sanity arms optional.\n\n### 3.5 Offline weight-presence assumption and surrogate fallback\n\n**Assumption (A9).** Model weights are assumed pre-cached on local disk at `/workspace/models/<slot_id>/`. Without this, no real LLM can exercise the protocol and only the surrogate fallback runs.\n\n**Surrogate fallback.** When Slot-1 or Slot-2 fails lock-in, the design substitutes a deterministic rule-based **surrogate scorer** with a logistic-regression-style model fit on researcher-hand-scored name features (Tier-A frequency counts, bigram overlap with the parent's surname, gender, era alignment). Surrogate parameters are calibrated once at lock-in (researcher scores a calibration subset of ~20 names by hand) and then frozen. The surrogate score for each trial is a probability that deterministically selects the highest-probability option from the candidate set; the resulting top-1 accuracy is reported as a **pipeline feasibility check only**, never as evidence about any LLM. The surrogate arm is **explicitly flagged in the output JSON** as `\"arm\": \"surrogate\"` (versus `\"arm\": \"model_slot_1\"` or `\"arm\": \"model_slot_2\"`) so the reader can never confuse it with real-model evidence.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Prompt and Scoring Protocol\n\n### 4.1 Primary neutral prompt template\n\n```\nYou will be shown a person and a relation query. Select the single\nbest answer from the seven options.\n\nPerson: {QUERY_NAME}\nQuery:  Who is {QUERY_NAME}'s {ROLE}?\n\nA) {OPT_A}\nB) {OPT_B}\nC) {OPT_C}\nD) {OPT_D}\nE) {OPT_E}\nF) {OPT_F}\nG) {OPT_G}\n\nReply with one letter only, A through G.\n```\n\n`{ROLE}` is `\"parent\"` in the C→P direction and `\"child\"` in the P→C direction. All other tokens are identical between directions except `{QUERY_NAME}` and the option list — this is the **parallel-prompt** property required for the directional contrast.\n\n### 4.2 Sanity-check prompt variants\n\nThe primary neutral prompt is paired with two short sanity-check variants to detect prompt framing effects:\n\n- **Role-cued variant.** `Query: {QUERY_NAME}'s {ROLE} is which of:` — syntactically tighter, no question mark.\n- **Terse variant.** `Who is {QUERY_NAME}'s {ROLE}? A) ... G) ...` — single line, no preamble.\n\nVariants are **sanity checks only**: a small between-variant discrepancy (|ΔDAI| ≤ 4 pp on a deliberately lenient tolerance) is required by the analysis plan to confirm prompt framing does not by itself manufacture directional bias. The pre-registered decision rule does not condition on these variants; they exist simply to bound prompt-cueing artifacts.\n\n### 4.3 Response parsing\n\n- *Step R1.* Strip whitespace; take the leftmost occurrence of a single uppercase letter `A`–`G`. If absent, mark `\"unparseable\"` and treat as **incorrect** for top-1.\n- *Step R2.* If the response contains more than one letter and parsing is ambiguous (e.g., `\"A or B\"`), record `\"ambiguous\"` and treat as **incorrect for top-1**, but separately retain the response for a qualitative audit.\n- *Step R3.* A trial is **correct** iff the parsed letter equals the gold letter exactly.\n\n### 4.4 k-way MCQ candidate set construction\n\n- k = 7.\n- 1 gold + 6 distractors.\n- For C→P trials the option set is 1 gold parent + 6 distractor *parents*. For P→C trials it is 1 gold child + 6 distractor *children*. The role of the option set is held constant within each direction so that the model's option-utility metrics are not confounded across directions.\n- **Distractor sampling rule.** Distractors are drawn from a *role-matched pool* built by the generator: parents in C→P, children in P→C. Within the role-matched pool, sample 6 names matched on the gold entity's `(era, tier, gender)` stratum — `gender` here is the gender of the gold (a mother-trial distractors come from the same-gender parent pool). Sampling uses a fixed seed derived from `hash(pair_id) XOR direction_index` so the trial is reproducible.\n- **Distractor pool sizing.** Each (era × tier × role × gender) stratum must have at least 7 entries for a gold at that stratum; the generator emits a `pairs_audit.json` warning if any gold needs a smaller stratum (sampled without replacement within the pool that exists, but logged).\n- **Random order.** Within each trial, the 7 options are presented in an independently shuffled order seeded by `hash(pair_id, direction_index, model_slot_index, prompt_variant_id)`.\n\n### 4.5 Scoring metrics (definitions)\n\nFor each (slot, direction, prompt_variant) cell:\n\n- **Top-1 accuracy.** `Acc_dir := (#correct trials) / (#trials)`.\n- **MRR.** For an MCQ, the model's response is a single letter; MRR reduces to 1 if top-1 is correct, else 0. We additionally record a **ranked-MRR** if the model emits anything other than a single letter (e.g., a token list), using the gold letter's order in the token stream; this is a tertiary metric and is not the primary outcome.\n\nThe **primary outcome** is **Acc** per direction, from which **DAI** is computed.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Controls and Stratification\n\n### 5.1 Inclusion / exclusion rules (logged)\n\n**Inclusion** requires all of:\n- Both parent and child appear in `fame_tiers.json` at **tier A, B, or C** (i.e., have a tier rationale).\n- `relationship` is unambiguously **biological**, verified by researcher notes.\n- Researcher `confidence ≥ 4` (or `confidence = 5` if either member is tier C; see §5.3).\n- The Q3 gold-child tie-break resolves to exactly one child (i.e., the eldest-in-table child is uniquely defined; ambiguous DoB → exclusion).\n\n**Exclusion buckets** (each with a label that is logged into `exclusion_reason`):\n- `non_biological_step` (step-parent or step-child).\n- `non_biological_adopted` (adoptive relationships).\n- `non_biological_half_sibling_incident` (illegal pair generation if cross-labelled, or parent–half-sibling).\n- `non_biological_in_law` (in-laws).\n- `contested_paternity` (researcher coulId not verify biology).\n- `tier_resolution_failure` (one member missing from `fame_tiers.json`).\n- `gold_tiebreak_ambiguous` (DoB ambiguous between two in-table children).\n- `distractor_pool_too_small` (matched-pool size < 7; trial-level exclusion only, pair stays in pool for future scaling).\n\n### 5.2 Fame tier (control dimension)\n\nTier enters the design at two points:\n1. **Distractor matching (§4.4)**: distractors are sampled from the same (era, tier, role, gender) stratum as the gold.\n2. **Sensitivity strata (§6)**: per-tier DAI is computed within tier-A pairs, tier-B, and tier-C; reported as sensitivity analyses.\n\nPre-registered decision rule: the directional hypothesis counts as **supported** only if the **tier-A DAI** is positive and the sign test rejects H0 within tier-A. Tier-B and tier-C are reported but are not pre-registered gates (smaller N reduces power).\n\n### 5.3 Era\n\nEra ∈ {`pre_1980`, `1980_2010`, `post_2010`}. Era is matched within the distractor pool and reported as a covariate in the per-stratum analysis. The `tier_C_requires_top_confidence` rule (§5.1) reflects the observation that pre-1980 era mixed with tier C is the most error-prone combination.\n\n### 5.4 Gender\n\nGender of the parent and child are recorded but only used (a) for distractor matching in the same role-directions and (b) as a sensitivity stratum (`parent_gender × child_gender` cell-wise DAI). The design does **not** pre-register gender as a primary gate; it is exploratory.\n\n### 5.5 Multi-child parents (locked tie-break)\n\n**Locked rule (Q3).** For each parent, the gold child is determined by:\n\n> *The eldest child of the parent who appears in the `fame_tiers.json` table (at any tier A/B/C), deterministically chosen by date of birth.*\n\nThis same gold child is used as the gold in **both** the C→P and P→C trials for that parent. Applying the rule symmetrically removes experimenter degrees of freedom and prevents gold selection from being conditioned on either direction's expected difficulty.\n\nIf a seed in `pairs_seed.jsonl` disagrees with the deterministic eldest-in-table child, the generator prefers the deterministic choice, logs the disagreement in `pairs_audit.json`, and **does not silently overwrite** the seed — disagreement is an auditable event, not a hidden edit.\n\n### 5.6 Alias handling\n\nEach pair is tested under **canonical names only** in the primary arm to keep the design clean. An optional secondary arm (run only if budget permits per §3.4) re-runs a small sub-sample (~25% of pairs) under `(full legal name, common first name, stage name)` for both members, and the resulting DAI is compared to the primary arm's DAI as an alias-sensitivity check. Aliases are sourced from `aliases[]` in `fame_tiers.json`.\n\n### 5.7 Deceased status\n\n`deceased` is recorded for both members and reported as a sensitivity stratum (`parent_deceased × child_deceased`) within the exploratory analyses; it is not a pre-registered gate.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Analysis Plan\n\n### 6.1 Primary outcome and estimator\n\nFor each model slot, the primary outcome is:\n\n> **DAI := Acc_C→P − Acc_P→C**\n\ncomputed per (slot, prompt_variant). For the primary decision we use the **primary neutral prompt** cell.\n\n### 6.2 Confidence interval\n\nPaired bootstrap CI over pairs:\n\n- Resample pairs with replacement B = 10,000 times.\n- For each resample, recompute `Acc_C→P` and `Acc_P→C` from the resampled pairs and form `DAI*`.\n- Report the **percentile** 95% interval `[DAI*_(0.025), DAI*_(0.975)]`.\n\n**B-budget safeguard.** B = 10,000 is feasible at N ≤ 150 in stdlib (`random` with fixed seed per bootstrap iteration completes in seconds). If post-hoc sign-test p-values show extreme skew that warrants B = 100,000, the analysis script supports it via a CLI flag, and wall-clock remains < 30 s in stdlib.\n\n### 6.3 Inferential test\n\n**Paired two-sided sign test** on per-pair correctness flips:\n\n- For each pair, record whether the model is correct in C→P **and** correct in P→C; the pair contributes to the test only if the two outcomes **differ**.\n- Let `n_+ = #pairs where (C→P correct) ≠ (P→C correct) and C→P correct`.\n- Under H0, the number of `n_+` flips where C→P wins is Binomial(`n_+ + n_-`, 0.5); p-value is two-sided.\n- Report p-value next to DAI; the **sign test is the inferential test**, the bootstrap CI is the effect-size estimate.\n\n### 6.4 Sensitivity analyses\n\n- **Per-tier DAI:** DAI computed within tier-A, tier-B, tier-C separately; tier-A DAI is the pre-registered tier gate (see §5.2).\n- **Per-prompt-variant DAI:** |DAI_neutral − DAI_rolecued|, |DAI_neutral − DAI_terse|; **sanity-check tolerance ≤ 4 pp** for the primary direction to be considered **not driven by prompt framing**. A > 4 pp discrepancy is reported as a **prompt-cueing artifact** and downgrades the result to \"inconclusive due to prompt sensitivity.\"\n- **Alias arm DAI (if run):** reported but not pre-registered; large alias-induced shift is a **caveat**.\n\n### 6.5 Pre-registered decision rules\n\n| Outcome | Sign test p | Bootstrap 95% CI for DAI | Tier-A DAI | Prompt-cueing discrepancy | Verdict |\n|---------|-------------|--------------------------|------------|---------------------------|---------|\n| **Supported** | < 0.05 | excludes 0 | > 0 | ≤ 4 pp | Directional asymmetry confirmed. |\n| **Refuted** | < 0.05 | excludes 0 | < 0 | ≤ 4 pp | Directional asymmetry is reversed (children easier than parents). |\n| **Inconclusive — null** | ≥ 0.05 | overlaps 0 | any | any | Insufficient evidence either way at this N. |\n| **Inconclusive — prompt** | any | any | any | > 4 pp | Cannot rule out that the effect is a prompt-cueing artifact. |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Pre-registration Table\n\n| # | Pre-registered choice | Value |\n|---|------------------------|-------|\n| 1 | Model slot 1 | Concrete ≤ 8B IT family; checkpoint at lock-in (see §3.3). |\n| 2 | Model slot 2 | Distinct-vendor ≤ 8B IT family; checkpoint at lock-in (see §3.3). |\n| 3 | Base/IT contrast | **NOT pre-registered.** Optional exploratory extension only. |\n| 4 | Sample size N | **N = 100 pairs minimum; target N = 150.** |\n| 5 | Candidate set size k | **k = 7** (1 gold + 6 distractors). |\n| 6 | Distractor sampling rule | Matched on (era × tier × role × gender), seeded by `hash(pair_id, direction_index)`. |\n| 7 | Prompt template | Primary neutral (§4.1). Two sanity variants: role-cued, terse. |\n| 8 | Gold-child tie-break | **Eldest child of the parent appearing in `fame_tiers.json`**; applied symmetrically. |\n| 9 | Inclusion gates | biological + both in tier table + researcher confidence + unique tie-break. |\n| 10 | Exclusion buckets | step, adopted, half, in-law, contested, tier-resolution failure, ambiguous tie-break, small distractor pool (logged). |\n| 11 | Decoding | temperature 0.0, max_new_tokens 8, no sampling, 4-bit quant. |\n| 12 | Alias arm | Canonical-name only at primary; alias arm optional/secondary. |\n| 13 | Primary metric | **DAI = Acc_C→P − Acc_P→C.** |\n| 14 | Effect estimate | Paired bootstrap 95% percentile CI, **B = 10,000**. |\n| 15 | Inferential test | Paired two-sided sign test on per-pair correctness flips. |\n| 16 | Significance level α | **0.05**, two-sided. |\n| 17 | Power β | ≤ 0.20 (≥ 0.80 power) at Δ ≈ 12 pp with N = 100 (see §8). |\n| 18 | Pre-registered tier gate | Tier-A DAI must be > 0 for \"supported\". |\n| 19 | Pre-registered prompt-cueing tolerance | |DAI_neutral − DAI_variant| ≤ 4 pp. |\n| 20 | Sensitivity strata | Per-tier (A/B/C); per-prompt-variant; alias arm (if run). |\n| 21 | Random seed | `master_seed.txt` (64 hex), pinned at lock-in. |\n| 22 | Hardware ceiling | ≤ 8B params, 4-bit, CPU-only, ≤ 24 threads. |\n| 23 | Wall-clock budget | ≤ 3 hours total. |\n| 24 | Weight-presence assumption | A9 (see §3.5). Surrogate fallback engages when violated. |\n| 25 | Surrogate arm scope | Reported as `\"arm\": \"surrogate\"`; not used as evidence (pipeline feasibility only). |\n\nThe full pre-registration table is dumped to `/workspace/PRE_REGISTRATION.md` at lock-in (L3) for an audit trail.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Feasibility\n\n**Parameter ceiling.** The design is locked to **≤ 8B params, 4-bit quantization** for both slots. A 70B-class model is **infeasible** on the supplied 24-core CPU without a GPU within any reasonable wall-clock budget; it is therefore not part of the design.\n\n**Wall-clock budget.** §3.4 budget: ~1–1.5 h for both IT slots on the primary neutral prompt; ~2.5–3 h including the two prompt variants. The budget is the **≤ 3-hour wall-clock** cap pre-registered in row 23 of §7.\n\n**Power analysis.** For a paired two-sided sign test with N pairs and a true within-pair direction flip rate such that Δ ≈ 12 pp (the minimum detectable effect of interest), power ≥ 0.80 is achieved at N ≈ 100 under reasonable assumptions about baseline accuracy. At N = 150 the design is comfortably powered at Δ ≈ 10 pp. The target N = 150 is therefore the pre-registered headroom.\n\n**Explicit infeasibilities on this hardware.**\n- 70B-class model inference (CPU-only).\n- Large prompt sweeps beyond 3 prompt variants.\n- External data fetches (network disabled).\n- Training/fine-tuning of any model.\n- Real-time interactive probing (headless / batch only).\n\n**Surrogate fallback (re-stated).** When Slot-1 or Slot-2 weights are not pre-cached, a deterministic logistic-style surrogate calibrated on a researcher-scored subset (~20 names) takes the slot and is **labeled `\"arm\": \"surrogate\"`** — used only to verify the pipeline compiles and produces well-formed output, not as evidence about any LLM.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Threats to Validity and Limitations\n\n1. **Assumption locality (hypothesis not source-verified).** The directional-bias hypothesis is not source-supported offline because no citation could be fetched (network disabled). The design therefore treats the hypothesis as a **falsifiable pre-registered prior**, not as established prior; the decision rules of §6.5 make refutation and inconclusiveness first-class outcomes.\n\n2. **Unmodeled distractor-difficulty asymmetry.** The distractor pools are matched on (era × tier × role × gender), but residual name-frequency and within-tier popularity asymmetry may remain. This could differentially inflate or deflate accuracy in one direction. Mitigation: tier-A-only pre-registration gate pushes inference to the most difficulty-matched stratum; per-tier sensitivity analysis detects residual asymmetry.\n\n3. **Prompt-cueing artifacts.** Even with parallel prompts, lexical asymmetries (e.g., \"Who is {parent}'s child?\" in P→C may cue a single-named answer more than \"Who is {child}'s parent?\") could extract directional signal independent of model bias. Mitigation: pre-registered ≤ 4 pp tolerance on |ΔDAI| across prompt variants (§6.5 row 19).\n\n4. **Selection effects from the researcher-curated seed.** Favoring high-confidence pairs may skew the set toward longer-lived, older, or more polished celebrity dynasties. Mitigation: log the tier/gender/era distribution in `pairs_audit.json`; pre-registered tier-A gate limits the impact.\n\n5. **Biological-only inclusion may skew coverage.** Excluding step/adopted/half/in-law/contested relationships systematically removes some well-known celebrity dynasties. This is a **structural bias** in the stimulus set and is acknowledged; it increases the per-pair confidence but reduces the absolute factual coverage. Mitigation: reported as a limitation; future work could include these relationships as a separate arm.\n\n6. **MCQ format underestimates generative recall.** Forcing a letter answer cannot capture \"I do not know\" hesitations. The reported Acc may underestimate the model's true generative accuracy and may go in different directions for C→P vs. P→C if hesitation correlates with relation asymmetry. Mitigation: GP-style \"I don't know\" pass is not used in this design to keep it preregistered; the surrogate arm's calibration data preserves a calibrated baseline.\n\n7. **Model-weight local cache (A9).** The design assumes weights are pre-cached. If not, only the surrogate fallback can run; the report would still be pipeline-valid but not informative about LLMs. The researcher is responsible for confirming the cache before lock-in.\n\n8. **Single-decoding seed.** Because `temperature = 0`, there is no sampling-uncertainty in the model output; uncertainty in DAI therefore comes from pair resampling only. This is intentional and pre-registered, but it understates the full uncertainty if models had stochastic decoders.\n\n9. **Sample-size attrition.** If fewer than 100 pairs survive all inclusion/exclusion gates, the design is underpowered and the verdict defaults to \"inconclusive — null\" (per §6.5). The report documents the realized N rather than retroactively shrinking α.\n\n---\n\n## What Would Change If Executed\n\nIf this design were moved from offline design to executed experiment, the following actions would be required and are **not** part of this deliverable: (i) the researcher would author `/workspace/fame_tiers.json` and `/workspace/pairs_seed.jsonl` from public-knowledge celebrity pairs and tier rationales; (ii) the two model weights would be pre-cached under `/workspace/models/<slot_id>/` and locked in via the §3.3 procedure with frozen SHAs; (iii) `generate_pairs.py` would run to produce `pairs.jsonl`, `pairs_audit.json`, and `distractor_pools.json`; (iv) the MCQ scorer would produce per-trial parsed responses and top-1 outcomes; and (v) the analysis script would compute DAI, the 10,000-resample paired bootstrap CI, the sign test p-value, tier-A gate, and prompt-cueing tolerance and emit a verdict drawn from §6.5. None of these steps has been performed; this document is a complete design specification only, as required by the submission contract, and the directional-bias question remains open as a falsifiable pre-registered prior awaiting execution.\n", "evidence": "**Stages of approved approach carried into this document (each tied to a previous round):**\n- Plan round 1: scope (b), neutral prompts, closed-set k-way MCQ, paired directional contrast, DAI + bootstrap CI + sign test, 8 flagged assumptions A1–A8.\n- Plan round 2 (clarifications): (Q1) hybrid model-slot granularity, ≤8B param ceiling, A9 weight-caching assumption created; (Q2) IT-only primary, base/IT exploratory extension only; (Q3) \"eldest independently-verifiable celebrity on the fame-tier table\" locked with symmetric application; (Q4) static `/workspace/fame_tiers.json` table for stratification, inclusion gated separately.\n- All locked choices propagated verbatim to §1, §3, §5.5, §5.6, §7, §8.\n\n**Repository facts that bound the design (recap, no fabrication):**\n- `/workspace` contained only `task.json`; `track.md` did not exist as a file (its content lives inside `task.json` as the design-only constraint).\n- `python3 == 3.12.3`; no ML/NLP packages installed (`torch`, `transformers`, `vllm`, `numpy`, `scipy` etc. absent); `requests`/`urllib3` present but offline-only.\n- Network disabled (DNS failure to `pypi.org`).\n- `gpu_enabled: false`, `cpu_affinity_count: 24`.\n- These facts pin: ≤ 8B param ceiling, 4-bit quant, ≤ 3-hour wall-clock budget, stdlib-only analysis path, offline weight-presence assumption, surrogate fallback.\n\n**Constraints explicitly honored in the deliverable:**\n- No celebrity names invented (zero fictional pairs anywhere in the document).\n- No numeric results claimed (no accuracy figures, no p-values, no CIs reported as observed).\n- No claim that the experiment ran (closing paragraph restates design-only status).\n- Pre-registration table (§7) freezes every choice that §3/§5/§6 introduced.\n- Surrogate fallback is described and inconsistently labeled as `\"arm\": \"surrogate\"` to prevent reader confusion with real-model evidence.\n\n**No additional exploratory commands were run for this round** (the design is wholly derived from previously verified repository facts and the approved plan).", "assumptions": ["A9 (carried from plan round 2): model weights are pre-cached at /workspace/models/<slot_id>/ because GPU is off and the network is disabled; otherwise the design degrades to a surrogate pipeline-feasibility check (labelled as such and never as evidence).", "Wall-clock budget ≤ 3 hours is assumed on the supplied 24-core CPU hardware; not stated in the brief, so it is an assumption and a pre-registered cap.", "N = 100 pairs is informative at Δ ≈ 12 pp; N = 150 pairs is the headroom target. Both are power estimates based on the standard paired two-sided sign test; if realized N is below 100 the verdict defaults to \"inconclusive — null.\"", "Fame-tier rubric (A/B/C) operationalization is offered as a single deterministic researcher-seeded table at /workspace/fame_tiers.json with per-entry tier_rationale; tier is for stratification and matched-pair distractor sampling, inclusion is gated separately.", "The Q3 tie-break (\"eldest independently-verifiable celebrity child on the tier table\") is implementable in stdlib given a static in-repo tier table; if a parent's only in-table children's DoB is ambiguous, the pair is excluded, not silently tie-broken.", "The directional-bias hypothesis itself is not source-supported offline (network disabled); it is treated as a falsifiable pre-registered prior with explicit refutation and inconclusiveness branches, never as established prior.", "Stdlib-only analysis path is sufficient (random.seed for bootstrap, statistics for descriptive, hand-rolled paired sign test); no numpy/scipy was assumed to be available at execution time."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the researcher will allocate time and effort to author ≥ 100 high-confidence biological celebrity parent-child pairs into the seed; less than 100 pairs downgrades the verdict to inconclusive by design (not by redesign).", "Whether the researcher pre-caches both slot weights in 4-bit format compatible with the lock-in script; if not, surrogate fallback engages per slot independently.", "Whether the 5 pp prompt-cueing tolerance is set at a meaningful operational scale; the pre-registered value is 4 pp and is itself a design choice rather than an empirical calibration.", "Whether \"biological\" parentage definitions sufficiently cover edge cases (e.g., posthumously-conceived children of male celebrities born via surrogacy, contested-paternity names like surname genetics) — exclusions are logged but the final coverage depends on researcher notes.", "Whether the delivered /PRE_REGISTRATION.md lock-in artifact can be made append-only and verifiable after lock-in (depends on filesystem permissions; documented but not enforced in this design)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-f5e14f69-5822-46ca-94f2-c9ebff93a87d", "tool_call": {"id": "call-f5e14f69-5822-46ca-94f2-c9ebff93a87d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Submit full experimental design document\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experimental Design \\u2014 Directional Asymmetry in Celebrity Parent\\u2013Child Retrieval by LLMs (Design-Only)\\n\\n> **Scope.** This document is a complete, offline, design-only experimental protocol. No experiment has been executed. No celebrity names, no results, no reference numbers have been fabricated. Where the protocol depends on researcher input (seed lists, model checkpoints), the dependency is marked explicitly and the lock-in procedure is specified.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Hypothesis and Pre-registered Null\\n\\n**H1 (directional asymmetry).** For a held-out set of celebrity parent\\u2013child pairs, an instruction-tuned LLM achieves higher top-1 accuracy when asked to retrieve a **parent given a child** (Child\\u2192Parent; C\\u2192P) than when asked to retrieve a **child given a parent** (Parent\\u2192Child; P\\u2192C).\\n\\nFormally, with the **Directional Asymmetry Index**\\n\\n> **DAI := Acc_C\\u2192P \\u2212 Acc_P\\u2192C**\\n\\nthe pre-registered directional hypothesis is\\n\\n> **H1: DAI > 0**\\n\\n**H0 (null).**\\n\\n> **H0: DAI = 0**\\n\\ni.e., no direction effect: parents and children are equally retrievable in their respective directions.\\n\\n**Pre-registered statistical targets.**\\n- **Inferential test:** paired two-sided **sign test** on per-pair correct/incorrect outcomes across directions (\\u03b1 = 0.05, two-sided).\\n- **Effect estimate:** paired bootstrap CI for DAI over pairs (B = 10,000 resamples, pair as the resampling unit); 95% percentile interval.\\n- **Power target:** \\u03b2 \\u2264 0.20 (i.e., power \\u2265 0.80) for detecting a within-pair direction flip rate corresponding to **\\u0394 \\u2248 12 percentage points** in DAI.\\n- **Minimum pre-registered sample size:** **N = 100 pairs**. Target N = 150 pairs if seed authoring permits.\\n- **Decision rule (see \\u00a76):** *supported* requires the sign test p < 0.05 with the bootstrap CI for DAI excluding 0 and DAI > 0; *refuted* is the mirror of supported with DAI < 0; *inconclusive* otherwise. The hypotheses are intentionally a **falsifiable pre-registered prior**, not a source-supported prior (see \\u00a79 \\u2014 assumption locality).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Stimuli \\u2014 Files and Procedural Generator\\n\\nAll stimulus construction is offline, deterministic, and fully reproducible from researcher-authored seeds. No external data is fetched.\\n\\n### 2.1 File layout in `/workspace`\\n\\n| Path | Author | Role |\\n|------|--------|------|\\n| `/workspace/fame_tiers.json` | Researcher (hand-authored, deferred) | Static tier table used for **stratification only**. |\\n| `/workspace/pairs_seed.jsonl` | Researcher (hand-authored, deferred) | High-confidence parent\\u2013child pairs. |\\n| `/workspace/generate_pairs.py` | This protocol | Deterministic procedural generator. |\\n| `/workspace/pairs.jsonl` | Generator output | Final analysis-ready pair list. |\\n| `/workspace/pairs_audit.json` | Generator output | Audit / provenance log. |\\n| `/workspace/distractor_pools.json` | Generator output | Pre-built matched distractor pools per tier-pool. |\\n| `/workspace/master_seed.txt` | Researcher lock-in | 64-hex fixed seed; pins all randomness in the pipeline. |\\n| `/workspace/PRE_REGISTRATION.md` | Lock-in artifact | Frozen pre-registration decisions (dumped from \\u00a77). |\\n\\n### 2.2 `fame_tiers.json` schema (stratification table)\\n\\nEach entry is a single object with:\\n- `name` (string, canonical full name).\\n- `aliases[]` (list of strings; e.g., stage names, before/after marriage names).\\n- `gender` \\u2208 {`F`, `M`, `NB`, `UNK`}.\\n- `tier` \\u2208 {`A`, `B`, `C`} with `tier_rationale` (one short clause, hand-authored).\\n- `domain` \\u2208 {`film`, `music`, `sports`, `politics`, `business`, `royalty`, `other`}.\\n- `era` \\u2208 {`pre_1980`, `1980_2010`, `post_2010`}.\\n- `deceased` (bool) and `dob`, `dod` (ISO dates; `null` if unknown).\\n\\nInclusion in the tier table requires at minimum a viable `tier_rationale`. Tier assignment rubric (offline, deterministic, lock-in):\\n- **Tier A.** Held a top-tier public leadership role in a baseline reference year (e.g., Forbes Celebrity 100 baseline), OR head-of-state, OR lifetime / hall-of-fame inductee of a major domain.\\n- **Tier B.** Major-award nominee or winner (Grammy/Oscar/Emmy-equivalent) OR a top-charting release in a baseline year OR recurring leading role in a flagship series.\\n- **Tier C.** Durable public profile but not meeting A or B (e.g., mid-tier release, supporting roles, generational recognition by other criteria).\\n\\nInclusion into the **pair set** is *not* gated by tier alone (see \\u00a75.1). Tier only governs distractor-pool sampling and per-tier sensitivity strata.\\n\\n### 2.3 `pairs_seed.jsonl` schema (researcher-authored)\\n\\nOne JSON object per line:\\n- `pair_id` (string, `p0001`, \\u2026).\\n- `parent_name` (string, must match a `name` in `fame_tiers.json`).\\n- `gold_child_name` (string, must match a `name` in `fame_tiers.json`; the resolution rule in \\u00a75.5 below is also enforced by the generator as a cross-check).\\n- `relationship` must equal `\\\"biological\\\"` (any other value triggers exclusion; see \\u00a75.6).\\n- `confidence` \\u2208 {1,2,3,4,5} (researcher self-rated; pairs with `confidence < 4` are flagged for audit, not silently dropped).\\n- `parent_dob`, `child_dob` (ISO date).\\n- `notes` (free text; e.g., references to ambiguous paternity \\u2014 these pairs are still excluded by \\u00a75.6).\\n\\n### 2.4 `generate_pairs.py` \\u2014 deterministic procedural generator\\n\\nThe generator reads the two seed files and `master_seed.txt` and emits three artifacts. Pseudocode:\\n\\n```\\nseeds   = read_jsonl(\\\"pairs_seed.jsonl\\\")\\ntiers   = read_json(\\\"fame_tiers.json\\\")\\nrng     = seeded_rng(\\\"master_seed.txt\\\")\\n\\n# 1. Apply inclusion gates.\\nfor s in seeds:\\n    if s.relationship != \\\"biological\\\":  exclude(reason=\\\"non_biological\\\")\\n    if s.parent_name not in tiers:      exclude(reason=\\\"parent_not_in_tier_table\\\")\\n    if s.gold_child_name not in tiers:  exclude(reason=\\\"child_not_in_tier_table\\\")\\n    if min(parent_tier, child_tier) == \\\"C\\\" and s.confidence < 5:\\n        exclude(reason=\\\"tier_C_requires_top_confidence\\\")\\n\\n# 2. Apply Q3 gold-child tie-break (defensive cross-check).\\n#    Gold child must be the eldest of parent's children who are in fame_tiers.json.\\n#    If the seed's gold_child_name disagrees with the deterministic eldest-in-table\\n#    child, log the disagreement and prefer the deterministic choice (logged, not silent).\\n\\n# 3. Pre-build distractor pools by (role, era, tier, gender).\\nbuild_distractor_pools(tiers, rng) -> \\\"distractor_pools.json\\\"\\n\\n# 4. Emit analysis-ready pairs.\\nemit_pairs_jsonl(...)\\nemit_audit_json(...)\\n```\\n\\n### 2.5 `pairs.jsonl` schema (analysis-ready)\\n\\n- `pair_id`\\n- `parent_name`, `parent_gender`, `parent_tier`, `parent_era`, `parent_deceased`\\n- `gold_child_name`, `child_gender`, `child_tier`, `child_era`, `child_deceased`\\n- `aliases_parent[]`, `aliases_child[]`\\n- `relationship` \\u2208 {`biological`}\\n- `inclusion_reason` (e.g., `\\\"both_tier_or_better\\\"`, `\\\"tier_C_top_confidence\\\"`)\\n- `exclusion_reason` (only present if excluded; otherwise `null`)\\n- `tier_pool` (the (era \\u00d7 tier \\u00d7 role) stratum used for distractor sampling)\\n- `prompt_variant_seed` (fixed hash for trial-level randomness)\\n- `gold_tiebreak_method` (`\\\"eldest_in_table\\\"`, always)\\n\\n### 2.6 `pairs_audit.json` schema (provenance)\\n\\n- `generator_version` (semver, frozen)\\n- `master_seed_sha256`\\n- `seeds_in`, `seeds_out_after_gates`\\n- `tier_distribution` (histogram `{A,B,C}` for parents and children)\\n- `era_distribution` (histogram for parents and children)\\n- `gender_cross_table` (parent_gender \\u00d7 child_gender counts)\\n- `exclusion_histogram` (counts by `exclusion_reason`)\\n- `inter_pool_distractor_count_min`, `_max` (distractor-pool sizes; detect empty pools)\\n- `tiebreak_disagreements` (list of seeds where seed gold \\u2260 deterministic eldest-in-table; kept but resolved to deterministic)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Models and Querying\\n\\n### 3.1 Model slots (pre-registered, primary)\\n\\n| Slot | Role | Scale | Status |\\n|------|------|-------|--------|\\n| **Slot-1** | Concrete instruction-tuned family (deferred checkpoint) | \\u2264 8B params, 4-bit | **Pre-registered** |\\n| **Slot-2** | Distinct-vendor instruction-tuned family at the same scale | \\u2264 8B params, 4-bit | **Pre-registered** |\\n\\nConcretely, Slot-1 is named as a candidate **Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct**-class family and Slot-2 as a candidate **Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct**-class family. **Final checkpoint choice is deferred to the lock-in procedure (see \\u00a73.3).** The pair is selected to (a) be at \\u22648B parameter scale for CPU feasibility, (b) come from distinct pretraining-token corpora to mitigate same-family leakage, and (c) be released under a permissive license so weights can be redistributed offline.\\n\\n### 3.2 Decoding settings\\n\\n- `temperature = 0.0` (greedy; deterministic; reproducibility).\\n- `max_new_tokens = 8` (a single letter response plus stopping margin).\\n- `repetition_penalty = 1.0` (no penalty; MCQ format leaves no role for repetition).\\n- `do_sample = False`.\\n- Quantization: 4-bit weight loading via `bitsandbytes`-style scheme if available; pure FP16 fallback documented.\\n- Context: prompt only (\\u2264 600 tokens), no chat-template tokens added that would shift presented-options order.\\n- Batching: trial-level `batch_size = 1`; parallel trials fanned out across `cpu_affinity_count = 24` worker processes.\\n\\n### 3.3 Lock-in procedure for exact checkpoints\\n\\nBecause checkpoints cannot be downloaded (network disabled) and weights cannot pre-exist by construction of this design, the lock-in is a *human-in-the-loop* procedure:\\n\\n1. **Step L1.** Researcher places pre-cached weight directories for **both** slots at `/workspace/models/<slot_id>/` (e.g., `/workspace/models/slot1/snapshots/\\u2026`).\\n2. **Step L2.** Researcher runs `lock_in_check.py`, which:\\n    - asserts the directory exists and `/workspace/models/slot1/config.json` declares `hidden_size` and parameter count \\u2264 8B;\\n    - asserts there exists at least one file matching `*.safetensors` or `*.bin`;\\n    - prints the model's `model_type`, parameter count, and SHA-256 of `config.json`;\\n    - prompts the researcher to type `LOCK <slot_id>` on stderr to confirm.\\n3. **Step L3.** On LOCK, the lock-in script appends the model's identifier and SHA-256 to `PRE_REGISTRATION.md` and freezes the slot. After lock-in, no slot substitution is permitted without a re-pre-registration entry.\\n4. **Step L4.** The same procedure is repeated independently for Slot-2.\\n\\nIf lock-in fails for a slot, the slot is reported as **unrunnable** and the surrogate fallback (\\u00a73.5) is engaged for that slot only.\\n\\n### 3.4 Hardware-runtime budget breakdown\\n\\n| Phase | Estimated wall-clock (24 CPUs, no GPU, 4-bit) |\\n|-------|---------------------------------------------|\\n| Lock-in check (L1\\u2013L4) | < 1 minute (file enumeration only). |\\n| Generator (deterministic) | < 30 seconds. |\\n| Per-model MCQ scoring, N=100 pairs \\u00d7 2 directions | ~20\\u201340 minutes (\\u2248 5\\u201315 tok/sec, \\u22648B params, 4-bit). |\\n| Per-model MCQ scoring, prompt-variant arms (\\u00d72 variants \\u00d7 2 directions \\u00d7 N) | ~40\\u201380 minutes. |\\n| Analysis (bootstrap, sign tests, sensitivity) | < 5 minutes (stdlib only; B=10,000 over N=100). |\\n| **Total for both IT slots, single prompt** | ~1\\u20131.5 hours. |\\n| **Total for both IT slots, primary + 2 prompt variants** | ~2.5\\u20133 hours. |\\n\\nThe pre-registered budget is **\\u2264 3 hours wall-clock**, leaving the prompt-variant sanity arms optional.\\n\\n### 3.5 Offline weight-presence assumption and surrogate fallback\\n\\n**Assumption (A9).** Model weights are assumed pre-cached on local disk at `/workspace/models/<slot_id>/`. Without this, no real LLM can exercise the protocol and only the surrogate fallback runs.\\n\\n**Surrogate fallback.** When Slot-1 or Slot-2 fails lock-in, the design substitutes a deterministic rule-based **surrogate scorer** with a logistic-regression-style model fit on researcher-hand-scored name features (Tier-A frequency counts, bigram overlap with the parent's surname, gender, era alignment). Surrogate parameters are calibrated once at lock-in (researcher scores a calibration subset of ~20 names by hand) and then frozen. The surrogate score for each trial is a probability that deterministically selects the highest-probability option from the candidate set; the resulting top-1 accuracy is reported as a **pipeline feasibility check only**, never as evidence about any LLM. The surrogate arm is **explicitly flagged in the output JSON** as `\\\"arm\\\": \\\"surrogate\\\"` (versus `\\\"arm\\\": \\\"model_slot_1\\\"` or `\\\"arm\\\": \\\"model_slot_2\\\"`) so the reader can never confuse it with real-model evidence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Prompt and Scoring Protocol\\n\\n### 4.1 Primary neutral prompt template\\n\\n```\\nYou will be shown a person and a relation query. Select the single\\nbest answer from the seven options.\\n\\nPerson: {QUERY_NAME}\\nQuery:  Who is {QUERY_NAME}'s {ROLE}?\\n\\nA) {OPT_A}\\nB) {OPT_B}\\nC) {OPT_C}\\nD) {OPT_D}\\nE) {OPT_E}\\nF) {OPT_F}\\nG) {OPT_G}\\n\\nReply with one letter only, A through G.\\n```\\n\\n`{ROLE}` is `\\\"parent\\\"` in the C\\u2192P direction and `\\\"child\\\"` in the P\\u2192C direction. All other tokens are identical between directions except `{QUERY_NAME}` and the option list \\u2014 this is the **parallel-prompt** property required for the directional contrast.\\n\\n### 4.2 Sanity-check prompt variants\\n\\nThe primary neutral prompt is paired with two short sanity-check variants to detect prompt framing effects:\\n\\n- **Role-cued variant.** `Query: {QUERY_NAME}'s {ROLE} is which of:` \\u2014 syntactically tighter, no question mark.\\n- **Terse variant.** `Who is {QUERY_NAME}'s {ROLE}? A) ... G) ...` \\u2014 single line, no preamble.\\n\\nVariants are **sanity checks only**: a small between-variant discrepancy (|\\u0394DAI| \\u2264 4 pp on a deliberately lenient tolerance) is required by the analysis plan to confirm prompt framing does not by itself manufacture directional bias. The pre-registered decision rule does not condition on these variants; they exist simply to bound prompt-cueing artifacts.\\n\\n### 4.3 Response parsing\\n\\n- *Step R1.* Strip whitespace; take the leftmost occurrence of a single uppercase letter `A`\\u2013`G`. If absent, mark `\\\"unparseable\\\"` and treat as **incorrect** for top-1.\\n- *Step R2.* If the response contains more than one letter and parsing is ambiguous (e.g., `\\\"A or B\\\"`), record `\\\"ambiguous\\\"` and treat as **incorrect for top-1**, but separately retain the response for a qualitative audit.\\n- *Step R3.* A trial is **correct** iff the parsed letter equals the gold letter exactly.\\n\\n### 4.4 k-way MCQ candidate set construction\\n\\n- k = 7.\\n- 1 gold + 6 distractors.\\n- For C\\u2192P trials the option set is 1 gold parent + 6 distractor *parents*. For P\\u2192C trials it is 1 gold child + 6 distractor *children*. The role of the option set is held constant within each direction so that the model's option-utility metrics are not confounded across directions.\\n- **Distractor sampling rule.** Distractors are drawn from a *role-matched pool* built by the generator: parents in C\\u2192P, children in P\\u2192C. Within the role-matched pool, sample 6 names matched on the gold entity's `(era, tier, gender)` stratum \\u2014 `gender` here is the gender of the gold (a mother-trial distractors come from the same-gender parent pool). Sampling uses a fixed seed derived from `hash(pair_id) XOR direction_index` so the trial is reproducible.\\n- **Distractor pool sizing.** Each (era \\u00d7 tier \\u00d7 role \\u00d7 gender) stratum must have at least 7 entries for a gold at that stratum; the generator emits a `pairs_audit.json` warning if any gold needs a smaller stratum (sampled without replacement within the pool that exists, but logged).\\n- **Random order.** Within each trial, the 7 options are presented in an independently shuffled order seeded by `hash(pair_id, direction_index, model_slot_index, prompt_variant_id)`.\\n\\n### 4.5 Scoring metrics (definitions)\\n\\nFor each (slot, direction, prompt_variant) cell:\\n\\n- **Top-1 accuracy.** `Acc_dir := (#correct trials) / (#trials)`.\\n- **MRR.** For an MCQ, the model's response is a single letter; MRR reduces to 1 if top-1 is correct, else 0. We additionally record a **ranked-MRR** if the model emits anything other than a single letter (e.g., a token list), using the gold letter's order in the token stream; this is a tertiary metric and is not the primary outcome.\\n\\nThe **primary outcome** is **Acc** per direction, from which **DAI** is computed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Controls and Stratification\\n\\n### 5.1 Inclusion / exclusion rules (logged)\\n\\n**Inclusion** requires all of:\\n- Both parent and child appear in `fame_tiers.json` at **tier A, B, or C** (i.e., have a tier rationale).\\n- `relationship` is unambiguously **biological**, verified by researcher notes.\\n- Researcher `confidence \\u2265 4` (or `confidence = 5` if either member is tier C; see \\u00a75.3).\\n- The Q3 gold-child tie-break resolves to exactly one child (i.e., the eldest-in-table child is uniquely defined; ambiguous DoB \\u2192 exclusion).\\n\\n**Exclusion buckets** (each with a label that is logged into `exclusion_reason`):\\n- `non_biological_step` (step-parent or step-child).\\n- `non_biological_adopted` (adoptive relationships).\\n- `non_biological_half_sibling_incident` (illegal pair generation if cross-labelled, or parent\\u2013half-sibling).\\n- `non_biological_in_law` (in-laws).\\n- `contested_paternity` (researcher coulId not verify biology).\\n- `tier_resolution_failure` (one member missing from `fame_tiers.json`).\\n- `gold_tiebreak_ambiguous` (DoB ambiguous between two in-table children).\\n- `distractor_pool_too_small` (matched-pool size < 7; trial-level exclusion only, pair stays in pool for future scaling).\\n\\n### 5.2 Fame tier (control dimension)\\n\\nTier enters the design at two points:\\n1. **Distractor matching (\\u00a74.4)**: distractors are sampled from the same (era, tier, role, gender) stratum as the gold.\\n2. **Sensitivity strata (\\u00a76)**: per-tier DAI is computed within tier-A pairs, tier-B, and tier-C; reported as sensitivity analyses.\\n\\nPre-registered decision rule: the directional hypothesis counts as **supported** only if the **tier-A DAI** is positive and the sign test rejects H0 within tier-A. Tier-B and tier-C are reported but are not pre-registered gates (smaller N reduces power).\\n\\n### 5.3 Era\\n\\nEra \\u2208 {`pre_1980`, `1980_2010`, `post_2010`}. Era is matched within the distractor pool and reported as a covariate in the per-stratum analysis. The `tier_C_requires_top_confidence` rule (\\u00a75.1) reflects the observation that pre-1980 era mixed with tier C is the most error-prone combination.\\n\\n### 5.4 Gender\\n\\nGender of the parent and child are recorded but only used (a) for distractor matching in the same role-directions and (b) as a sensitivity stratum (`parent_gender \\u00d7 child_gender` cell-wise DAI). The design does **not** pre-register gender as a primary gate; it is exploratory.\\n\\n### 5.5 Multi-child parents (locked tie-break)\\n\\n**Locked rule (Q3).** For each parent, the gold child is determined by:\\n\\n> *The eldest child of the parent who appears in the `fame_tiers.json` table (at any tier A/B/C), deterministically chosen by date of birth.*\\n\\nThis same gold child is used as the gold in **both** the C\\u2192P and P\\u2192C trials for that parent. Applying the rule symmetrically removes experimenter degrees of freedom and prevents gold selection from being conditioned on either direction's expected difficulty.\\n\\nIf a seed in `pairs_seed.jsonl` disagrees with the deterministic eldest-in-table child, the generator prefers the deterministic choice, logs the disagreement in `pairs_audit.json`, and **does not silently overwrite** the seed \\u2014 disagreement is an auditable event, not a hidden edit.\\n\\n### 5.6 Alias handling\\n\\nEach pair is tested under **canonical names only** in the primary arm to keep the design clean. An optional secondary arm (run only if budget permits per \\u00a73.4) re-runs a small sub-sample (~25% of pairs) under `(full legal name, common first name, stage name)` for both members, and the resulting DAI is compared to the primary arm's DAI as an alias-sensitivity check. Aliases are sourced from `aliases[]` in `fame_tiers.json`.\\n\\n### 5.7 Deceased status\\n\\n`deceased` is recorded for both members and reported as a sensitivity stratum (`parent_deceased \\u00d7 child_deceased`) within the exploratory analyses; it is not a pre-registered gate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Analysis Plan\\n\\n### 6.1 Primary outcome and estimator\\n\\nFor each model slot, the primary outcome is:\\n\\n> **DAI := Acc_C\\u2192P \\u2212 Acc_P\\u2192C**\\n\\ncomputed per (slot, prompt_variant). For the primary decision we use the **primary neutral prompt** cell.\\n\\n### 6.2 Confidence interval\\n\\nPaired bootstrap CI over pairs:\\n\\n- Resample pairs with replacement B = 10,000 times.\\n- For each resample, recompute `Acc_C\\u2192P` and `Acc_P\\u2192C` from the resampled pairs and form `DAI*`.\\n- Report the **percentile** 95% interval `[DAI*_(0.025), DAI*_(0.975)]`.\\n\\n**B-budget safeguard.** B = 10,000 is feasible at N \\u2264 150 in stdlib (`random` with fixed seed per bootstrap iteration completes in seconds). If post-hoc sign-test p-values show extreme skew that warrants B = 100,000, the analysis script supports it via a CLI flag, and wall-clock remains < 30 s in stdlib.\\n\\n### 6.3 Inferential test\\n\\n**Paired two-sided sign test** on per-pair correctness flips:\\n\\n- For each pair, record whether the model is correct in C\\u2192P **and** correct in P\\u2192C; the pair contributes to the test only if the two outcomes **differ**.\\n- Let `n_+ = #pairs where (C\\u2192P correct) \\u2260 (P\\u2192C correct) and C\\u2192P correct`.\\n- Under H0, the number of `n_+` flips where C\\u2192P wins is Binomial(`n_+ + n_-`, 0.5); p-value is two-sided.\\n- Report p-value next to DAI; the **sign test is the inferential test**, the bootstrap CI is the effect-size estimate.\\n\\n### 6.4 Sensitivity analyses\\n\\n- **Per-tier DAI:** DAI computed within tier-A, tier-B, tier-C separately; tier-A DAI is the pre-registered tier gate (see \\u00a75.2).\\n- **Per-prompt-variant DAI:** |DAI_neutral \\u2212 DAI_rolecued|, |DAI_neutral \\u2212 DAI_terse|; **sanity-check tolerance \\u2264 4 pp** for the primary direction to be considered **not driven by prompt framing**. A > 4 pp discrepancy is reported as a **prompt-cueing artifact** and downgrades the result to \\\"inconclusive due to prompt sensitivity.\\\"\\n- **Alias arm DAI (if run):** reported but not pre-registered; large alias-induced shift is a **caveat**.\\n\\n### 6.5 Pre-registered decision rules\\n\\n| Outcome | Sign test p | Bootstrap 95% CI for DAI | Tier-A DAI | Prompt-cueing discrepancy | Verdict |\\n|---------|-------------|--------------------------|------------|---------------------------|---------|\\n| **Supported** | < 0.05 | excludes 0 | > 0 | \\u2264 4 pp | Directional asymmetry confirmed. |\\n| **Refuted** | < 0.05 | excludes 0 | < 0 | \\u2264 4 pp | Directional asymmetry is reversed (children easier than parents). |\\n| **Inconclusive \\u2014 null** | \\u2265 0.05 | overlaps 0 | any | any | Insufficient evidence either way at this N. |\\n| **Inconclusive \\u2014 prompt** | any | any | any | > 4 pp | Cannot rule out that the effect is a prompt-cueing artifact. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Pre-registration Table\\n\\n| # | Pre-registered choice | Value |\\n|---|------------------------|-------|\\n| 1 | Model slot 1 | Concrete \\u2264 8B IT family; checkpoint at lock-in (see \\u00a73.3). |\\n| 2 | Model slot 2 | Distinct-vendor \\u2264 8B IT family; checkpoint at lock-in (see \\u00a73.3). |\\n| 3 | Base/IT contrast | **NOT pre-registered.** Optional exploratory extension only. |\\n| 4 | Sample size N | **N = 100 pairs minimum; target N = 150.** |\\n| 5 | Candidate set size k | **k = 7** (1 gold + 6 distractors). |\\n| 6 | Distractor sampling rule | Matched on (era \\u00d7 tier \\u00d7 role \\u00d7 gender), seeded by `hash(pair_id, direction_index)`. |\\n| 7 | Prompt template | Primary neutral (\\u00a74.1). Two sanity variants: role-cued, terse. |\\n| 8 | Gold-child tie-break | **Eldest child of the parent appearing in `fame_tiers.json`**; applied symmetrically. |\\n| 9 | Inclusion gates | biological + both in tier table + researcher confidence + unique tie-break. |\\n| 10 | Exclusion buckets | step, adopted, half, in-law, contested, tier-resolution failure, ambiguous tie-break, small distractor pool (logged). |\\n| 11 | Decoding | temperature 0.0, max_new_tokens 8, no sampling, 4-bit quant. |\\n| 12 | Alias arm | Canonical-name only at primary; alias arm optional/secondary. |\\n| 13 | Primary metric | **DAI = Acc_C\\u2192P \\u2212 Acc_P\\u2192C.** |\\n| 14 | Effect estimate | Paired bootstrap 95% percentile CI, **B = 10,000**. |\\n| 15 | Inferential test | Paired two-sided sign test on per-pair correctness flips. |\\n| 16 | Significance level \\u03b1 | **0.05**, two-sided. |\\n| 17 | Power \\u03b2 | \\u2264 0.20 (\\u2265 0.80 power) at \\u0394 \\u2248 12 pp with N = 100 (see \\u00a78). |\\n| 18 | Pre-registered tier gate | Tier-A DAI must be > 0 for \\\"supported\\\". |\\n| 19 | Pre-registered prompt-cueing tolerance | |DAI_neutral \\u2212 DAI_variant| \\u2264 4 pp. |\\n| 20 | Sensitivity strata | Per-tier (A/B/C); per-prompt-variant; alias arm (if run). |\\n| 21 | Random seed | `master_seed.txt` (64 hex), pinned at lock-in. |\\n| 22 | Hardware ceiling | \\u2264 8B params, 4-bit, CPU-only, \\u2264 24 threads. |\\n| 23 | Wall-clock budget | \\u2264 3 hours total. |\\n| 24 | Weight-presence assumption | A9 (see \\u00a73.5). Surrogate fallback engages when violated. |\\n| 25 | Surrogate arm scope | Reported as `\\\"arm\\\": \\\"surrogate\\\"`; not used as evidence (pipeline feasibility only). |\\n\\nThe full pre-registration table is dumped to `/workspace/PRE_REGISTRATION.md` at lock-in (L3) for an audit trail.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility\\n\\n**Parameter ceiling.** The design is locked to **\\u2264 8B params, 4-bit quantization** for both slots. A 70B-class model is **infeasible** on the supplied 24-core CPU without a GPU within any reasonable wall-clock budget; it is therefore not part of the design.\\n\\n**Wall-clock budget.** \\u00a73.4 budget: ~1\\u20131.5 h for both IT slots on the primary neutral prompt; ~2.5\\u20133 h including the two prompt variants. The budget is the **\\u2264 3-hour wall-clock** cap pre-registered in row 23 of \\u00a77.\\n\\n**Power analysis.** For a paired two-sided sign test with N pairs and a true within-pair direction flip rate such that \\u0394 \\u2248 12 pp (the minimum detectable effect of interest), power \\u2265 0.80 is achieved at N \\u2248 100 under reasonable assumptions about baseline accuracy. At N = 150 the design is comfortably powered at \\u0394 \\u2248 10 pp. The target N = 150 is therefore the pre-registered headroom.\\n\\n**Explicit infeasibilities on this hardware.**\\n- 70B-class model inference (CPU-only).\\n- Large prompt sweeps beyond 3 prompt variants.\\n- External data fetches (network disabled).\\n- Training/fine-tuning of any model.\\n- Real-time interactive probing (headless / batch only).\\n\\n**Surrogate fallback (re-stated).** When Slot-1 or Slot-2 weights are not pre-cached, a deterministic logistic-style surrogate calibrated on a researcher-scored subset (~20 names) takes the slot and is **labeled `\\\"arm\\\": \\\"surrogate\\\"`** \\u2014 used only to verify the pipeline compiles and produces well-formed output, not as evidence about any LLM.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Threats to Validity and Limitations\\n\\n1. **Assumption locality (hypothesis not source-verified).** The directional-bias hypothesis is not source-supported offline because no citation could be fetched (network disabled). The design therefore treats the hypothesis as a **falsifiable pre-registered prior**, not as established prior; the decision rules of \\u00a76.5 make refutation and inconclusiveness first-class outcomes.\\n\\n2. **Unmodeled distractor-difficulty asymmetry.** The distractor pools are matched on (era \\u00d7 tier \\u00d7 role \\u00d7 gender), but residual name-frequency and within-tier popularity asymmetry may remain. This could differentially inflate or deflate accuracy in one direction. Mitigation: tier-A-only pre-registration gate pushes inference to the most difficulty-matched stratum; per-tier sensitivity analysis detects residual asymmetry.\\n\\n3. **Prompt-cueing artifacts.** Even with parallel prompts, lexical asymmetries (e.g., \\\"Who is {parent}'s child?\\\" in P\\u2192C may cue a single-named answer more than \\\"Who is {child}'s parent?\\\") could extract directional signal independent of model bias. Mitigation: pre-registered \\u2264 4 pp tolerance on |\\u0394DAI| across prompt variants (\\u00a76.5 row 19).\\n\\n4. **Selection effects from the researcher-curated seed.** Favoring high-confidence pairs may skew the set toward longer-lived, older, or more polished celebrity dynasties. Mitigation: log the tier/gender/era distribution in `pairs_audit.json`; pre-registered tier-A gate limits the impact.\\n\\n5. **Biological-only inclusion may skew coverage.** Excluding step/adopted/half/in-law/contested relationships systematically removes some well-known celebrity dynasties. This is a **structural bias** in the stimulus set and is acknowledged; it increases the per-pair confidence but reduces the absolute factual coverage. Mitigation: reported as a limitation; future work could include these relationships as a separate arm.\\n\\n6. **MCQ format underestimates generative recall.** Forcing a letter answer cannot capture \\\"I do not know\\\" hesitations. The reported Acc may underestimate the model's true generative accuracy and may go in different directions for C\\u2192P vs. P\\u2192C if hesitation correlates with relation asymmetry. Mitigation: GP-style \\\"I don't know\\\" pass is not used in this design to keep it preregistered; the surrogate arm's calibration data preserves a calibrated baseline.\\n\\n7. **Model-weight local cache (A9).** The design assumes weights are pre-cached. If not, only the surrogate fallback can run; the report would still be pipeline-valid but not informative about LLMs. The researcher is responsible for confirming the cache before lock-in.\\n\\n8. **Single-decoding seed.** Because `temperature = 0`, there is no sampling-uncertainty in the model output; uncertainty in DAI therefore comes from pair resampling only. This is intentional and pre-registered, but it understates the full uncertainty if models had stochastic decoders.\\n\\n9. **Sample-size attrition.** If fewer than 100 pairs survive all inclusion/exclusion gates, the design is underpowered and the verdict defaults to \\\"inconclusive \\u2014 null\\\" (per \\u00a76.5). The report documents the realized N rather than retroactively shrinking \\u03b1.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## What Would Change If Executed\\n\\nIf this design were moved from offline design to executed experiment, the following actions would be required and are **not** part of this deliverable: (i) the researcher would author `/workspace/fame_tiers.json` and `/workspace/pairs_seed.jsonl` from public-knowledge celebrity pairs and tier rationales; (ii) the two model weights would be pre-cached under `/workspace/models/<slot_id>/` and locked in via the \\u00a73.3 procedure with frozen SHAs; (iii) `generate_pairs.py` would run to produce `pairs.jsonl`, `pairs_audit.json`, and `distractor_pools.json`; (iv) the MCQ scorer would produce per-trial parsed responses and top-1 outcomes; and (v) the analysis script would compute DAI, the 10,000-resample paired bootstrap CI, the sign test p-value, tier-A gate, and prompt-cueing tolerance and emit a verdict drawn from \\u00a76.5. None of these steps has been performed; this document is a complete design specification only, as required by the submission contract, and the directional-bias question remains open as a falsifiable pre-registered prior awaiting execution.\\n\", \"evidence\": \"**Stages of approved approach carried into this document (each tied to a previous round):**\\n- Plan round 1: scope (b), neutral prompts, closed-set k-way MCQ, paired directional contrast, DAI + bootstrap CI + sign test, 8 flagged assumptions A1\\u2013A8.\\n- Plan round 2 (clarifications): (Q1) hybrid model-slot granularity, \\u22648B param ceiling, A9 weight-caching assumption created; (Q2) IT-only primary, base/IT exploratory extension only; (Q3) \\\"eldest independently-verifiable celebrity on the fame-tier table\\\" locked with symmetric application; (Q4) static `/workspace/fame_tiers.json` table for stratification, inclusion gated separately.\\n- All locked choices propagated verbatim to \\u00a71, \\u00a73, \\u00a75.5, \\u00a75.6, \\u00a77, \\u00a78.\\n\\n**Repository facts that bound the design (recap, no fabrication):**\\n- `/workspace` contained only `task.json`; `track.md` did not exist as a file (its content lives inside `task.json` as the design-only constraint).\\n- `python3 == 3.12.3`; no ML/NLP packages installed (`torch`, `transformers`, `vllm`, `numpy`, `scipy` etc. absent); `requests`/`urllib3` present but offline-only.\\n- Network disabled (DNS failure to `pypi.org`).\\n- `gpu_enabled: false`, `cpu_affinity_count: 24`.\\n- These facts pin: \\u2264 8B param ceiling, 4-bit quant, \\u2264 3-hour wall-clock budget, stdlib-only analysis path, offline weight-presence assumption, surrogate fallback.\\n\\n**Constraints explicitly honored in the deliverable:**\\n- No celebrity names invented (zero fictional pairs anywhere in the document).\\n- No numeric results claimed (no accuracy figures, no p-values, no CIs reported as observed).\\n- No claim that the experiment ran (closing paragraph restates design-only status).\\n- Pre-registration table (\\u00a77) freezes every choice that \\u00a73/\\u00a75/\\u00a76 introduced.\\n- Surrogate fallback is described and inconsistently labeled as `\\\"arm\\\": \\\"surrogate\\\"` to prevent reader confusion with real-model evidence.\\n\\n**No additional exploratory commands were run for this round** (the design is wholly derived from previously verified repository facts and the approved plan).\", \"assumptions\": [\"A9 (carried from plan round 2): model weights are pre-cached at /workspace/models/<slot_id>/ because GPU is off and the network is disabled; otherwise the design degrades to a surrogate pipeline-feasibility check (labelled as such and never as evidence).\", \"Wall-clock budget \\u2264 3 hours is assumed on the supplied 24-core CPU hardware; not stated in the brief, so it is an assumption and a pre-registered cap.\", \"N = 100 pairs is informative at \\u0394 \\u2248 12 pp; N = 150 pairs is the headroom target. Both are power estimates based on the standard paired two-sided sign test; if realized N is below 100 the verdict defaults to \\\"inconclusive \\u2014 null.\\\"\", \"Fame-tier rubric (A/B/C) operationalization is offered as a single deterministic researcher-seeded table at /workspace/fame_tiers.json with per-entry tier_rationale; tier is for stratification and matched-pair distractor sampling, inclusion is gated separately.\", \"The Q3 tie-break (\\\"eldest independently-verifiable celebrity child on the tier table\\\") is implementable in stdlib given a static in-repo tier table; if a parent's only in-table children's DoB is ambiguous, the pair is excluded, not silently tie-broken.\", \"The directional-bias hypothesis itself is not source-supported offline (network disabled); it is treated as a falsifiable pre-registered prior with explicit refutation and inconclusiveness branches, never as established prior.\", \"Stdlib-only analysis path is sufficient (random.seed for bootstrap, statistics for descriptive, hand-rolled paired sign test); no numpy/scipy was assumed to be available at execution time.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether the researcher will allocate time and effort to author \\u2265 100 high-confidence biological celebrity parent-child pairs into the seed; less than 100 pairs downgrades the verdict to inconclusive by design (not by redesign).\", \"Whether the researcher pre-caches both slot weights in 4-bit format compatible with the lock-in script; if not, surrogate fallback engages per slot independently.\", \"Whether the 5 pp prompt-cueing tolerance is set at a meaningful operational scale; the pre-registered value is 4 pp and is itself a design choice rather than an empirical calibration.\", \"Whether \\\"biological\\\" parentage definitions sufficiently cover edge cases (e.g., posthumously-conceived children of male celebrities born via surrogacy, contested-paternity names like surname genetics) \\u2014 exclusions are logged but the final coverage depends on researcher notes.\", \"Whether the delivered /PRE_REGISTRATION.md lock-in artifact can be made append-only and verifiable after lock-in (depends on filesystem permissions; documented but not enforced in this design).\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-3f01a50f-d724-4df6-86d5-49c35c5a1140", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
